	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048926769?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Long-distance cascade slurry pumping stations are energy-intensive systems whose operation must coordinate time-of-use electricity tariffs, inter-station storage, and safety-critical hydraulic constraints. This study proposes a multi-agent improved TD3 algorithm (MAITD3) under the centralized-training, decentralized-execution framework for cooperative scheduling of cascade slurry pumping stations. Monte Carlo dropout is used to estimate the predictive uncertainty of twin target critics, whose outputs are fused by inverse-variance weighting. A deterministic pump-decoding rule and a hard safety projection layer are further introduced to convert continuous flow commands into feasible pump operations while enforcing velocity and tank-level constraints. The method is validated on an industrial four-stage iron-concentrate slurry pipeline using five independent random seeds. Compared with MATD3, MAITD3 reduces the magnitude of the mean Q -value estimation bias from 1.254 to 0.394, the variance from 1.697 to 0.557, and the RMSE from 1.779 to 0.803. MAITD3 achieves a nominal mean cost saving of 6.43% relative to manual operation, while its best one-day trajectory achieves 9.65%. After accounting for terminal inventory imbalance, the corresponding closure-equivalent saving remains 6.58%–7.90%. The nominal best solution is 1.59% above the relaxed MILP reference, and all evaluated trajectories satisfy the modeled safety constraints. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of MAITD3 for safe and energy-cost-aware cooperative scheduling of cascade slurry pumping stations. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
