	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046154926?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Safety and predictability are important features to be considered when developing LLM agents as these types of systems acquire increasing control of the physical world infrastructure, persistent states, and other external means. One of the major flaws in existing agent architectures is low levels of safety due to excessive dependence on probability-based control and use of natural language instructions. This creates a requirement for a deterministic instruction layer. The Constraint Agent Execution Framework (CAEF) is introduced as a solution. CAEF operates as a deterministic model that implements a strict specification called Agent Instruction Language (AIL). Unlike soft borders, the CAEF uses constraint enforcement through transition validation, state-specific context, and limited actions. The use of both CAEF and AIL allows separating reasoning from control and providing a deterministic, audit-able, and safe execution system, keeping at the same time the benefits of flexible reasoning typical for LLMs. © 2026 IEEE.},
