	abstract = {The increasing deployment of unmanned aerial vehicles in logistics, surveillance, public safety, and urban monitoring is rapidly transforming low-altitude airspace into a dense and highly dynamic operational environment. Efficient management of such traffic is essential to prevent congestion, reduce delays, and avoid mid-air conflicts while maintaining energy efficiency and mission reliability. Traditional approaches, including centralized scheduling, rule-based navigation, and single-agent learning techniques, have been widely explored; however, they suffer from significant limitations. To address these challenges, this research proposes an intelligent drone traffic management framework based on multi-agent reinforcement learning, where each UAV acts as a decentralized decision-making agent capable of perceiving its surroundings and cooperating implicitly with neighboring drones. The proposed method integrates congestion-aware routing, adaptive altitude-layer allocation, real-time conflict avoidance, and energy-efficient trajectory planning within a unified learning architecture. The proposed framework enables learning emergent cooperative behaviors without the need for a global controller, thereby improving robustness and scalability in complex urban environments. Extensive simulation results demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed system compared with conventional centralized and rule-based methods. Quantitatively, the approach reduces average travel time by approximately 10-20 percent, decreases collision events per 1000 flights by more than 60 percent, and improves traffic throughput by nearly 15-25 percent in dense conditions. Performance is validated across multiple realistic scenarios, including dynamic no-fly zones, high-density traffic surges, and environmental disturbances, confirming strong robustness and adaptability. Overall, the proposed multi-agent learning framework provides an efficient, scalable, and safety-aware solution for intelligent drone traffic management and represents a promising step toward fully autonomous urban air mobility systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Conference paper},
