	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041911055?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Telemedicine faces persistent challenges in terms of communication efficiency, triage accuracy, and documentation burden. This study presents MEDCOMM-AGENT, a novel multi-agent AI architecture that addresses critical communication bottlenecks through hierarchical coordination and specialized agent collaboration. The system comprises four domain-specific agents: the Triage Intelligence Agent (TIA), Diagnostic Support Agent (DSA), Communication Facilitation Agent (CFA), and Documentation Synthesis Agent (DOCSA), orchestrated by a Supervisor Agent (SA) with structured debate protocols. We integrated advanced reasoning mechanisms, including ReAct patterns, iterative 4-cycle self-refinement, and CoT prompting, within a FHIR R4-compliant framework. Simulation-based evaluation across 1,500 synthetic encounters demonstrates significant improvements: 92.4% triage accuracy (95% CI: 90.1-94.3%, p<0.001) versus 34-68% baseline, 73% documentation time reduction (2.8 vs. 17.3 minutes), 41.1% diagnostic time improvement (10.6 vs. 18 minutes, Cohen's d=2.14), and 33.9% reduction in 30-day readmissions (RR = 0.66, p<0.001). Ablation studies revealed catastrophic triage degradation (-41.2%) without TIA, substantial diagnostic loss (-18.6%) without DSA, and 5.7-7.9% degradation across metrics without a supervisor. The architecture demonstrated robust performance under adversarial conditions (maximum <7% degradation under single stressors) with subadditive degradation under combined stressors. This study elevates patient communication to a status equal to diagnostic reasoning, providing a validated architectural framework and evaluation methodology for next-generation telemedicine. Computational analysis showed feasible deployment (23.0s latency, 0.70 cost, 11.8 GB memory) with linear scalability to 120 encounters/hour.  © 2026 IEEE.},
