	abstract = {As public agencies seek to reduce emissions and align with sustainability goals, the electrification of fleet vehicles has become a critical policy and operational focus. However, successful adoption of electric vehicles (EVs) in agency fleets depends not only on infrastructure and cost, but also on user attitudes, operational constraints, and organizational readiness. This study examines the concerns, attitudes, and organizational characteristics associated with EV adoption in Minnesota public agency fleets. A statewide survey of 86 agency personnel, spanning fleet managers, operations managers, engineers, and mechanics, was analyzed using exploratory factor analysis and logistic regression, and five follow-up interviews provided practitioner context. Organizational capacity (fleet size) was the strongest and most consistent predictor of EV adoption. Concerns about driving range, charging, maintenance, cost, and safety tended to be reported together, and higher overall concern was associated with lower adoption, though less consistently than fleet size. Respondents in agencies that already had EVs held more favorable attitudes towards the transition, while familiarity with EV technology showed a weaker and inconsistent association with adoption. These results suggest that pilot-vehicle programs and outreach that address concerns together, rather than charging infrastructure alone, may support fleet electrification, particularly for smaller or less-resourced agencies. © 2026 The Authors.},
