	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/APAN70967.2026.11709667},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055119422?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Decentralized swarm coordination requires local interaction rules that preserve collective motion while limiting collisions in dense or unstructured environments. Dynamic Emotional Field Swarming (DEFS) addresses this problem by assigning each agent a tri-variate [Curiosity, Fear, Affinity] state that adaptively modulates attraction, repulsion, and cohesion forces. The framework combines adaptive emotional modulation with an encounter-memory mechanism that discourages redundant local interactions and a Predictive Collision Anticipation (PCA) module that adapts agent behavior before potential collisions occur. We evaluate DEFS against baseline Boids in 20 paired trials and further examine ablation variants, parameter sensitivity, and comparisons with the Social Force Model to assess safety, exploration, and cohesion under matched simulation conditions. Against Boids (N=60, 800 steps, 20 trials), DEFS reduced collision rates from 5.682 ± 0.331% to 0.405 ± 0.112%. Ablation and comparison results clarify the contributions of predictive collision anticipation, encounter-memory adaptation, and emotional modulation to the observed improvement. The combined evidence indicates that DEFS prioritizes collision avoidance and decentralized coordination while accepting slower cohesion as a deliberate trade-off. Overall, DEFS provides a lightweight decentralized coordination framework that couples adaptive behavior with predictive collision avoidance.  © 2026 IEEE.},
