	pages = {20 - 36},
	doi = {10.1007/978-3-032-39395-1_2},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053581817?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Multi-Agent Path Finding (MAPF) is a critical challenge in robotics and logistics, characterized by high combinatorial complexity and the partial observability of real-world environments. Existing decentralized reinforcement learning (RL) methods often struggle with two primary bottlenecks: the emergence of egoistic agent behaviors that lead to frequent collisions, and a reliance on complex communication protocols that necessitate prohibitively long training durations. To mitigate these issues, we introduce an efficient decentralized planning framework based on the Decision Transformer (DT). By framing MAPF as a sequence modeling task and leveraging offline reinforcement learning, our approach reduces training time from weeks to hours while maintaining robust long-horizon credit assignment in sparse-reward environments. Furthermore, to address the inherent adaptability limitations of standard RL policies, we integrate a Large Language Model (GPT-4o) to provide high-level strategic guidance in dynamic settings. Experimental results demonstrate that our DT-based framework achieves performance parity with state-of-the-art benchmarks in static environments at a fraction of the computational cost. Additionally, the integration of GPT-4o improves adaptation in many dynamic goal-change settings, especially by reducing redirection latency after goal reassignment. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
