	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044578902?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Over the past decade, Artificial Intelligence (AI) has driven the rise of autonomous software agents across cyber and physical environments, tightly linked with the Internet of Things (IoT). In the cyber domain, agentic AI supports decision-making and information gathering, while AI agents such as belief-desire-intention (BDI) ones manage production and shop-floor systems, often connected to industrial IoT (IIoT) infrastructures. As these agents grow more autonomous and integrated, system complexity and risk will increase. Cyber agents may generate biased or inaccurate results due to hallucinations and shallow reasoning while physical BDI agents face environmental uncertainties from sensor noise and actuator flaws. When combined, these risks can amplify and cause severe failures. Their autonomy should therefore be strengthened using logical frameworks and model-driven engineering, and their operation supervised via rule-based control to ensure safety and reliability. This vision paper proposes a rule-based supervision method to enhance the reliability of agent-based systems both consist of agentic AI and AI agents. By integrating fuzzy, epistemic, subjective, and probabilistic logics within a multi-logic modelling (MLM) framework, the approach supports hybrid reasoning under diverse uncertainties. The MLM framework enables coherent integration of multiple logical perspectives, allowing verification and monitoring of agent behaviour through rule-based control. Ultimately, the goal is to build trustworthy and explainable agent systems via MPM.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
	type = {Conference paper},
