	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105055659467?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Reliable random access for Internet of Things (IoT) nodes in real wireless environments is important for communication-layer availability and reliability in Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) systems. This paper implements and evaluates a distributed random access mechanism based on the Exponential-weight algorithm for Exploration and Exploitation in a multi-agent setting, referred to as EXP3-MA, on a real testbed built with ESP32 devices and ESP-NOW. In the implemented system, the access point (AP) organizes communication using a slotted frame structure and feeds back the channel state to user nodes after each slot, including three possible outcomes: idle, successful transmission, and collision. User nodes independently update their access strategies according to the received slot-level feedback, while the AP does not perform learning or centralized scheduling. The implementation addresses practical issues that are often abstracted away in simulations, including frame synchronization, slot-level result delivery, and user-side online learning on constrained embedded devices. Experimental results show that the implemented learning-based random access mechanism achieves higher slot utilization and cumulative average throughput than classical Slotted ALOHA under real wireless conditions, while controlled-interference experiments illustrate its behavior under access-layer availability degradation. The results provide an embedded feasibility study and practical insights for deploying learning-based random access mechanisms in resource-constrained IoT systems. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2027.},
