	abstract = {Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being integrated into cybersecurity applications to enhance threat detection, Security Operations Center (SOC) automation, vulnerability analysis, and cyber–physical system protection. Their capacity for contextual reasoning and interaction with external tools has enabled the emergence of agentic artificial intelligence systems capable of supporting complex cyber-defence workflows. However, as LLM-based systems evolve from analytical assistants to autonomous agents capable of tool invocation and multi-agent coordination, new challenges arise in reasoning reliability, execution safety, coordination stability, and governance compliance. Existing surveys typically examine these aspects independently, resulting in a fragmented understanding of autonomous LLM-driven cyber defence. This paper presents a PRISMA-guided systematic review of 144 peer-reviewed SCI/SCIE-indexed studies published between 2021 and 2025. To provide a unified analytical perspective, we propose a four-dimensional taxonomy that classifies systems according to cybersecurity task domains, autonomy levels, agentic and multi-agent architectures, and tooling-safety-governance integration. The analysis reveals that while LLMs demonstrate strong analytical capabilities, most systems remain confined to low-autonomy configurations and lack robust safety and coordination mechanisms. Based on these findings, we identify key research challenges and outline future directions for developing verifiable, governance-aware, and operationally reliable autonomous cyber-defence systems for real-world SOC environments. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
