	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041154998?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The Collaborative Task Sequencing and Multi-Agent Path Finding (CTS-MAPF) problem requires agents to accomplish sequences of tasks while avoiding collisions, posing significant challenges due to its combinatorial complexity. This work introduces CTS-PLL, a hierarchical framework that extends the configuration-based CTS-MAPF planning paradigm with two complementary mechanisms for improved robustness and anytime solution quality. First, when the configuration-based planner encounters prolonged stagnation, a lock detection and release module constructs an auxiliary MAPF instance from the current CTS state and invokes complete MAPF method for recovery. Second, after a feasible plan is obtained for a candidate joint task sequence, an intra-sequence refinement procedure is applied to further improve path quality. Extensive evaluations across sparse and dense benchmarks demonstrate that CTS-PLL achieves state-of-the-art performance in terms of success rate and solution quality, while maintaining competitive runtime efficiency. Real-world robot experiments further demonstrate the feasibility of the approach in practice.  © 2026 IEEE.},
