	abstract = {Maritime transport forms the backbone of global trade, where safety, sustainability, and operational efficiency are central priorities. However, seafarers' working conditions have frequently lagged international norms. To remedy these gaps, the International Labour Organization (ILO) introduced the Maritime Labour Convention 2006, which came into force in 2013 and established minimum standards for seafarers' health, safety, and welfare, serving as a vital complement to SOLAS and MARPOL. This study examines deficiencies and detentions related to seafarers' working and living conditions as recorded during Port State Control (PSC) inspections. To identify the key determinants of these detentions, a hybrid analytical framework was employed: the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE) to address data imbalance, Logistic Regression (LR) to determine statistically significant predictors, and Bayesian Network (BN) modeling to reveal probabilistic relationships among variables. The findings show that older vessels (over 10 years) and larger ships face a higher likelihood of deficiencies and subsequent detentions. The most recurrent issues involve unpaid or delayed wages, inadequate cleanliness and hygiene conditions, and non-compliance with work/rest hour regulations. These results highlight the need for more targeted inspection planning, enhanced inspector training, and improved maintenance and risk-management practices, particularly for aging fleets. Strengthening global inspection consistency is crucial both to protect seafarers’ rights and to promote safer, more sustainable maritime operations. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
