	abstract = {Task specialization can enable robot teams to work more efficiently. However, it usually requires substantial engineering effort to decompose a task, design subtask controllers, and assign roles. Natural collectives suggest that specialization can emerge through adaptive behavior as groups scale or density increases. In this paper, we study the emergence of task specialization in an evolutionary multi-robot setting. Our example task is object retrieval, which allows for two interdependent subtasks. We study a homogeneous multi-robot system and evolve artificial neural networks as robot controllers. Without assigning subtasks or rewarding specialization, we observe emergent task specialization. Our results show a density-dependent transition from predominantly generalist behavior at low densities to emergent task specialization at higher densities. Given that our robots have no memory, their initial positions act as an implicit task-allocation cue. Together, these results provide a model of how division of labor can emerge from density-dependent task ecology, embodiment, and interaction constraints, bridging biological and robotic perspectives on multi-agent organization. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
