	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048688827?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The Model Context Protocol (MCP) has emerged as the de facto standard for connecting Large Language Models (LLMs) to external data and tools, effectively functioning as the “USB-C for Agentic AI.” As MCP adoption accelerates in enterprise environments, a critical knowledge gap exists: there is no comprehensive framework for understanding how security and safety threats manifest in this decentralized, protocol-driven ecosystem. This Systematization of Knowledge (SoK) addresses that gap by providing a unified taxonomy of risks in the MCP ecosystem, distinguishing between adversarial security threats (e.g., indirect prompt injection, tool poisoning, supply-chain attacks) and epistemic safety hazards (e.g., alignment failures in distributed tool delegation, hallucination-driven execution errors). We analyze the structural vulnerabilities of MCP primitives—specifically Resources, Prompts, and Tools—and demonstrate how context can be weaponized to trigger unauthorized operations in multi-agent environments. We further survey state-of-the-art defenses, ranging from cryptographic provenance (ETDI) to runtime intent verification and zero-trust architecture, and conclude with a roadmap for securing the transition from conversational chatbots to autonomous agentic operating systems. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
