	abstract = {We estimate the probability distribution of the net costs of decommissioning a first-generation offshore wind farm in the German North Sea. The process-based Monte Carlo model covers a 288 MW reference wind farm with 80 monopile-founded 3.6 MW turbines and ten scenarios. The model is based on expert judgments and market data from a multi-year research project with industry partners. It models dependence among cost rates and among process durations and quantities. It links offshore removal to onshore processing costs and material revenues. Expected net costs, defined as gross expenditures less material revenues, range from €242,000 to €545,000 per MW in 2022 prices. Correlation among inputs raises the upper tail far more than the mean. The 95th percentile of the baseline lies 34% above the mean, versus 23% for independent inputs. The decommissioning concepts filed with the German regulator include risk surcharges of up to 30%. Such a surcharge covers the 95th percentile only for independent inputs. Material revenues fall short of onshore costs in 15% of the baseline simulations. Leaving the scour protection in situ lowers expected costs by 18%, and cutting the foundation above the seabed lowers them by a further 9 percentage points. Feeder logistics raise costs by 48% for foundations and by 65% for foundations and turbines, conditional on the fleet, the 2022 vessel market, and the implicit treatment of weather. For the 3.6 MW reference case, the scenario ranking is the same under all stochastic specifications tested. The results provide a benchmark for financial provisioning. The Author This is an open access article under the CC BY license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},
