	abstract = {The state-space explosion problem severely limits the application of formal verification in large-scale industrial maintenance and production systems. This work introduces a novel trace-driven verification algorithm and a bounded-bottleneck detection methodology that enable interactive verification and anomaly diagnosis of industrial-scale high-level Petri net (HLPN) models, serving as a simulation-based framework for maintenance modeling. To translate these formal mathematical models into practical engineering decision support, we implement these methodologies within a multi-agent simulation architecture based on coordinated software agents. A central manager coordinates specialized tool agents that autonomously detect threshold-based bottlenecks, operational livelocks, and system deadlocks via natural language queries. The framework was validated across three use cases representing realistic industrial maintenance processes, ranging from a baseline loop to a full-scale simulation of a preventive maintenance irradiation facility. Simulation results demonstrate that while lightweight local AI inference models exhibit instability during function calling, advanced large-scale Large Language Model (LLM) architectures maintain consistent diagnostic performance in extensive industrial verification contexts. The proposed framework demonstrates how formal modeling and simulation methods can be operationalized as an integrated technology layer for scalable decision support in complex production systems. The study concludes that human-in-the-loop agentic workflows remain essential for ensuring safe and reliable maintenance decision-making in safety-critical industrial environments. The Authors This is an open access article under the CC BY license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/},
