	abstract = {The rapid growth of e-commerce has increased pressure on warehouses to process orders faster and at lower cost. Robotic Mobile Fulfillment Systems (RMFS), where mobile robots transport storage pods to stationary pickers, help improve warehouse efficiency but rely on two closely related decisions: pod sequencing, which determines the order in which pods are delivered, and rack repositioning, which determines where pods are stored after picking. Poor pod sequencing can delay pickers by making them wait for needed pods, while ineffective repositioning increases future retrieval distances and robot congestion, reducing overall system performance. Optimizing these decisions together is difficult because the number of possible pod sequences grows rapidly with problem size, and repositioning must adapt in real time to changing demand and traffic conditions. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a deep reinforcement learning (DRL) approach for adaptive rack repositioning that learns effective storage policies from simulated multi-robot interactions without relying on fixed heuristic rules. The proposed method is implemented within a multi-fidelity simulation framework. A genetic algorithm first evaluates pod sequencing options using a low-fidelity surrogate model, while a high-fidelity simulator provides detailed training feedback by capturing robot congestion and collision avoidance. A composite reward function is designed to balance travel efficiency and future demand awareness. Experimental results across 25 warehouse configurations show that the proposed approach significantly reduces robot travel distance compared with rule-based and practical baseline methods, with improvements validated through Wilcoxon statistical tests. Additional makespan experiments under different fleet sizes further demonstrate significant reductions in order completion time over all rule-based baselines. The framework also achieves over 94% computational savings compared with optimization using only high-fidelity simulation, demonstrating the scalability and effectiveness of the multi-fidelity simulation optimization approach for joint pod sequencing and rack repositioning in dynamic RMFS environments. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
