	abstract = {Given that multi-manipulator coal-gangue sorting robots suffer from low cooperative control efficiency and subpar dynamic adaptability, this article puts forth an improved MADDPG-based coordinated method for multiple manipulators. The working area of the manipulators is established to define their operational ranges, with each manipulator acting as an independent agent. An improved Actor-Critic framework featuring centralized training and decentralized execution is involved to construct a multi-agent reinforcement learning system, which accomplishes unified training and validation of manipulators. The proposed IM-MADDPG incorporates spatiotemporal safety constraints and an attention-based dynamic task allocation mechanism, which effectively mitigates inter-manipulator collisions and enhances coordination efficiency in heterogeneous multi-robot systems. Compared to existing MADDPG variants, our method emphasizes both safety assurance and adaptive role assignment, leading to superior performance in dynamic environments. During action decision-making, information sharing and coordination strategies among agents dynamically adjust manipulator motion postures and spatial trajectories based on real-time states, adapting to the sorting of irregular gangue in intricate coal mining circumstances. Network parameters are refined by virtue of experience replay sampling and gradient descent algorithms, so that training stability and convergence speed are bolstered. As manifested by the experimental results, this approach boosts the precision and efficiency for multi-manipulator cooperative control, attaining a task completion rate of 97.5% in static conditions and 91.5% in dynamic scenarios. It is able to efficiently mitigate hindrance and allocate tasks in dynamic convoluted environments, boasting persistent optimization of control strategies through real-time inter-agent information exchange. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data minin