	abstract = {Continuous operation of automated guided vehicles (AGVs) in intelligent factories is often interrupted by battery depletion and additional travel to fixed charging stations. Although wireless power transfer (WPT) can reduce physical connector wear and enable flexible charging, its use for AGV energy support must account for charging efficiency loss, uncrewed aerial vehicle (UAV) hovering consumption, propulsion energy, and operational safety constraints. This work develops a physically parameterized hybrid aerial-ground energy-management framework in which UAVs equipped with WPT modules (UWs) provide supplementary and on-demand charging support to AGV fleets. The coupled UW-AGV charging mission is formulated as an episodic Markov decision process. To support real-time decision-making, a feature-based expected SARSA (FES) algorithm is developed to compress the state-action space through five problem-specific feature functions associated with energy cost, downtime penalty, charging efficiency, spatial proximity, and battery-safety margin. The proposed model explicitly incorporates WPT efficiency, UAV hovering energy, and propulsion-related energy consumption. Simulation experiments compare the proposed FES algorithm with feature-based SARSA, rule-based, and random baselines, and further analyze convergence behavior, computational scalability, UW deployment density, and the sensitivity of system performance to WPT efficiency, UW hovering or docking-maintenance power, and propulsion energy. The results show that FES provides computationally efficient and interpretable scheduling for hybrid AGV-UAV energy coordination, while limitations of large-scale UAV-assisted WPT deployment are explicitly discussed.  © 2016 IEEE.},
