	abstract = {Decentralized multi-robot coordination is a knowledge-driven problem that requires scalable goal allocation, adaptive reasoning, and robustness to dynamic and uncertain environments. We propose I-GNN, a decentralized algorithmic coordination framework that integrates Graph Neural Network (GNN)-based goal assignment with D* Lite-based incremental path replanning. During training, obstacle-aware path costs computed via D* Lite-based incremental path planning are transformed into soft, cost-weighted supervisory distributions derived from Hungarian cost-optimal matching. This dual-assignment formulation enables the GNN to encode relative path feasibility rather than rely on hard optimal labels. At runtime, decentralized GNN inference performs scalable goal allocation without global synchronization or centralized optimization. To address dynamic disturbances, the framework incorporates temporal obstacle-decay modeling and event-triggered reassignment, enabling stable coordination under transient occlusions, congestion, and simulated robot failures. The proposed framework is evaluated in a hospital-inspired occupancy grid testbed under dynamic and partially observable conditions. Experimental results demonstrate high decentralized assignment accuracy (97–99%) with Hungarian-derived cost-optimal assignments under obstacle-aware path costs, low reassignment frequency, and effective collision avoidance across varying robot team sizes. Comparative analysis against centralized Hungarian assignment and hybrid RRT–DWA planning shows improved scalability and robustness within the adopted simulation protocol. These results validate I-GNN as a decentralized coordination framework that combines cost-aware optimization principles with learning-based multi-agent reasoning. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
