	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053032583?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Production LLM-agent frameworks rely on control primitives—human-in-the-loop approval gates, cancellation, and timeouts—to enforce a barrier: while a run is paused, cancelled, or timed out, no gated side effect reaches the world. Whether shipping primitives enforce this has gone unmeasured; it holds on none of six open-source frameworks. Model-free differential probes isolate a sibling leak—an approval gate suspends its branch while a sibling’s effect executes during the pause—in every framework with a pre-execution gate (five of six: four independent designs plus the Node port of one, two runtimes), and confirm replay double-execution, cancellation orphans, and timeout zombies. The hazard is reachable: frontier models emit the leak-triggering plan shape at up to 14% on authored multi-effect tasks (on naturalistic τ-bench episodes they predominantly serialize, the gap latent by model habit, not absent by construction), and live models driving unmodified frameworks leak 215/1,200 runs (P(leak | emitted)=1.00); a 13-incident public corpus corroborates replay and cancellation failures. We repair the gap with SoundGate, an environment-external Rust reference monitor at the tool boundary, enforcing hold-until-decided, reject-cancels, dedup-on-replay, and fence-on-cancel under a stated complete-mediation contract. The admission core’s model is mechanically verified (Verus; TLA+/TLC to 7.5×10<sup>7</sup> states; TLAPS) and bridged to the deployed Rust by Loom exploration and differential conformance over 1.2×10<sup>7</sup> operations with zero divergences—refinement evidence, not a refinement proof. SoundGate blocks every measured violation on all six frameworks while releasing approved effects—gated τ-bench episodes complete with zero fail-closed refusals—at ∼1 ms per admitted write. © 2013 IEEE.},
