	abstract = {To address the core limitations of Deep Deterministic Policy Gradient (DDPG) algorithms in warehouse logistics robot navigation—specifically, inadequate temporal memory, inaccurate Q-value evaluation, and low training sample efficiency—this paper proposes a novel multidimensional optimization scheme. The main novelties include: (1) Integrating Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks into the Actor-Critic framework to enhance historical state utilization and decision continuity; (2) Adopting an improved dueling network architecture to decouple the Q-value into state value and action advantage, thereby refining critic evaluation accuracy; and (3) Designing a dynamic dual-pool experience replay mechanism that prioritizes high-value samples based on TD-error and reward dimensions to optimize training efficiency. These three components form a synergistic optimization system. Experimental results demonstrate that the optimized algorithm significantly outperforms the original DDPG. It achieved a trajectory coherence index of 0.9 (a 38 % improvement) and an obstacle avoidance success rate of 83 %. The dueling network reduced the Q-value mean squared error to 2 × 10<sup>−3</sup>, while the dynamic dual-pool mechanism increased key sample utilization to 89.3 % and accelerated convergence speed by 38 %. In practical tests, the algorithm exhibited a dynamic obstacle avoidance response time of 210 ms, a path optimality index of 0.95, and a multi-robot task delay of only 5.2 s. The proposed multidimensional optimization strategy significantly enhances the adaptability, reliability, and navigation efficiency of warehouse logistics robots in complex dynamic environments, providing a robust solution for intelligent warehousing systems. Additionally, we discuss safety/ethical requirements and deployment scalability (compute and communication constraints) for large robot fleets. © 2026},
