	abstract = {In automated warehouse systems, efficient multi-robot task allocation (MRTA) and collision-free navigation constitute critical challenges, often modeled as the Multi-Agent Pickup and Delivery (MAPD) problem. Auction-based task assignment methods enable distributed decision-making but frequently lead to path conflicts and deadlocks in narrow corridors, causing system-wide throughput degradation. Although centralized planning methods can ensure deadlock-free operation, they often sacrifice path efficiency and scalability. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a semaphore-based algorithm utilizing signal reservation mechanisms to coordinate corridor access and prevent deadlock occurrences. Simulation experiments conducted in both sparse and dense warehouse layouts demonstrate that the proposed approach effectively eliminates deadlocks, reduces unnecessary detours, and maintains competitive time efficiency compared to priority-based replanning methods. The results validate the practicality of semaphore mechanisms for real-time MAPD systems and provide a scalable solution for deadlock-free navigation in high-density robotic environments. © 2026 IEEE.},
