	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053566532?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This work presents a comparative evaluation of the incremental D* Lite algorithm applied to collision-free trajectory planning in multi-agent mobile robotic systems. The proposed methodology integrates image processing techniques and graph theory to generate discretized navigable environments and model dynamic scenarios with multiple agents interacting simultaneously. The system employs a decentralized collision-avoidance strategy based on dynamic cost penalization using Euclidean safety distances between agents. When a potential conflict is detected, D* Lite performs incremental local replanning by updating only the affected regions of the environment, thereby avoiding global recomputations. The performance of the algorithm is quantitatively compared with the classical Dijkstra and A* approaches using metrics such as computation time, number of expanded nodes, and trajectory length. Experimental validations were carried out in MATLAB using multiple maze-like scenarios with mobile agents interacting simultaneously. The experimental results show that D* Lite reduces planning time by more than 80% compared to Dijkstra and by more than 50% compared to A*, while also significantly decreasing the number of expanded nodes and the computational effort associated with search-space exploration, maintaining feasible and near-optimal trajectories. These results confirm that the incremental approach of D* Lite constitutes an efficient alternative for autonomous multi-agent navigation in partially known and dynamic environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
