	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036275469?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Coordinating heterogeneous robot teams for efficient, full-area coverage in unknown environments demands robust, fully autonomous control. Outer-planet exploration such as missions to Mars exemplifies this challenge, requiring reliable autonomy to guarantee mission success. To overcome the difficulties of decentralized, heterogeneous multi-agent coordination, this paper presents Centralized Feedback-Driven Reward Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (CFR-MARL), a decentralized multi-agent reinforcement learning framework designed for cooperative coverage path planning in heterogeneous teams of unmanned ground vehicles (UGVs) and an unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV). The CFR-MARL integrates a Value Decomposition Network (VDN) for centralized training with potential-based reward shaping, delivering dense, team-level feedback while enabling fully decentralized execution with minimal communication overhead. Through a shared discretized map, the UAV performs real-time obstacle detection, coverage validation, and energy logistics, dynamically guiding UGVs to unexplored areas and preventing redundant traversal. Simulation results demonstrate that CFR-MARL substantially accelerates learning, reduces coverage overlap, and nearly eliminates mission failures due to collisions or energy depletion, while scaling effectively to larger UGV teams. These results establish the CFR-MARL as an efficient and scalable solution for resource-constrained planetary exploration and other heterogeneous robotic applications. © 2026 IAA. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
