	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045194521?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Legacy embedded firmware represents a critical security risk in modern infrastructure, yet traditional decompilation tools struggle with hardware-coupled code. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise in decompiling desktop software, their application to bare-metal firmware remains unexplored. We present a behavior-guided reverse engineering framework employing autonomous LLM agents that perform multi-step reasoning: reading disassembly, consulting MCU documentation, generating candidate code, compiling, simulating, analyzing trace differentials, and iteratively refining solutions across multiple attempts. This agentic approach, fundamentally different from single-shot API queries, enables the model to reason about complex firmware through tool use and self-correction. Using cycle-accurate simulation as our behavioral oracle, we evaluate two frontier coding agents, Claude Code (Opus 4.5) and OpenAI Codex (GPT-5.1-codex-max), across 52 real-world AVR firmware projects. Claude achieves a 54.4% metric-level pass rate compared to Codex's 37.0%, winning 41 of 52 head-to-head comparisons. Despite this, both agents exhibit systematic failures in safety-critical constructs: volatile qualifiers, interrupt handlers, and Harvard architecture memory access. Our findings reveal a critical "safety gap"where high syntactic accuracy masks deep behavioral flaws, demonstrating the need for human oversight and hardware-in-the-loop verification for firmware decompilation. While our empirical study focuses on the AVR architecture, the methodology generalizes to other embedded platforms and is the subject of ongoing work. © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Conference paper},
