	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045917961?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In multi-agent pickup and delivery (MAPD) systems, robot swarms must continuously respond to dynamically arriving tasks while ensuring collision-free action. Existing methods typically assume that the spatial distribution of tasks is uniform or random. However, real-world scenarios often exhibit spatial clustering patterns. To address this issue, we propose a novel algorithm, PHAsor-guided Adaptive Spatial task assignmEnt (PHASE). This is a three-stage decoupled framework. The first stage uses clustering based on the Complex Evacuation Guidance Map (CEGM) to match tasks with agents. The subsequent two stages employ the A* algorithm and reinforcement learning to allow each agent to learn distributed action policies. We extensively evaluate our algorithm against state-of-the-art baseline methods. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework is effective and generalizes well in complex, large-scale environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2027.},
