	abstract = {Multi-robot systems (MRS) are regarded as an ideal solution for material transportation tasks due to their advantages in flexibility and reliability. However, the presence of complex obstacles in manufacturing environments poses significant challenges to collision avoidance and path planning. This paper categorizes obstacles into surmountable and insurmountable types based on their reachable height relative to the payload and proposes a hierarchical obstacle crossing planner to address formation obstacle avoidance and navigation. Within this planner, a global agent guides the formation to avoid insurmountable obstacles and navigate toward the global target, and provides local targets to each robot. Local agents then direct each robot to avoid all obstacles and reach their local target. The planner is designed within a hierarchical multi-agent reinforcement learning (H-MARL) framework. To enable effective training, we develop the hierarchical cooperative agents with experience-sharing (HCAE) algorithm. This algorithm addresses inter-layer credit assignment through hetero-frequency actions and coordinates policy alignment among local agents via network and experience sharing. The planner is successfully trained in environments with randomly generated obstacles. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed planner effectively guides the formation through obstacle-dense regions, validating its robustness and generalization capability. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
