	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049704234?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Efficient motion planning for multi-node systems with rigid structural constraints remains a challenging problem in robotics and multi-agent coordination. This paper proposes a computational framework for real-time collision detection and path optimization in multi-node systems moving along constrained spiral trajectories. First, a time-driven motion model based on the Archimedean spiral is established to compute the continuous trajectories of interconnected nodes. A recursive geometric update mechanism enables efficient calculation of the spatial states of large-scale node chains. Second, an efficient collision detection algorithm based on vector cross-product tests is developed. By transforming structural interference into two-dimensional line segment intersection checks and integrating hierarchical time scanning with interval refinement, the method accurately identifies collision intervals while maintaining low computational complexity. Third, a path optimization strategy is introduced. A bisection search algorithm determines the minimum feasible spiral pitch satisfying collision constraints, while a multilayer perceptron (MLP) regression model predicts turning path length and guides optimal parameter selection. Simulation experiments demonstrate that the proposed framework accurately predicts collision moments (approximately 428.8 s) and determines the minimum feasible pitch (0.3936 m). Compared with traditional geometric traversal methods, the proposed approach significantly improves computational efficiency while maintaining high precision. These results indicate that the proposed framework provides a scalable and real-time solution for motion planning and collision avoidance in multi-node robotic systems and multi-agent trajectory planning scenarios.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
