	abstract = {Automated guided vehicles (AGVs) are crucial to the advancement of intelligent manufacturing systems. To ensure collision-free navigation for multiple robots in a warehouse environment, this study investigates the impact of time windows on multi-agent path finding and proposes a two-tier planning approach that integrates an enhanced A* algorithm with an effective conflict resolution strategy. In the first tier, to address the issue of excessive node exploration inherent in traditional A* algorithms due to the design of the heuristic function, we introduce a dynamic adjustment of the heuristic weight based on the distance between the current position and the target. This optimization significantly reduces the number of search nodes, thereby improving computational efficiency. Furthermore, a cubic B-spline curve is employed to smooth the generated paths, providing initial collision-free paths for multiple robots. In the second tier, we analyze various types of conflicts, including blocking conflicts, node conflicts, and position conflicts among the robots by utilizing a time-window graph representation of their paths. Based on this analysis, appropriate decoupling strategies are developed to effectively resolve these conflicts. Finally, simulation results demonstrate that the proposed method successfully generates low-cost, smooth, and collision-free navigation paths for multi-robot systems, thereby improving the overall performance of AGV systems in dynamic environments. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
