	abstract = {Multi-Agent Path Finding (MAPF) aims to efficiently compute conflict-free paths for multiple agents operating in a shared environment. This work focuses on the Lifelong variant of MAPF, where agents continuously receive new tasks upon completing the current ones. Recent studies have increasingly explored distributed and locally observable MAPF settings, as they better reflect real-world conditions in which robots often lack global information and suffer from intermittent communication. However, most existing approaches largely overlook practical energy constraints, limiting their deployability in warehouse and factory scenarios. To this end, we introduce modeling mechanisms such as electricity quantity, electricity consumption, and charging stations in the local observation grid environment. We further propose an energy-aware MAPF method that integrates heuristic planning with deep reinforcement learning: a global-level heuristic planner decides whether an agent should continue heading toward its task goal or detour to a charging station, while a local learning-based policy handles collision avoidance and path execution. The experimental results show that in scenarios where actual energy consumption needs to be considered, the method significantly reduces the low-power operation time while maintaining task throughput. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2027.},
