	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105023839321?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Autonomous underwater vehicles (AUVs), equipped with fins and rudders as primary control mechanisms, are indispensable tools for deep-sea exploration and underwater acoustic data acquisition. However, the distributed navigation and planning of multiple AUVs remain constrained by inherent limitations in power efficiency, maneuverability, and coordination. This study presents an innovative collision avoidance strategy tailored to multi-AUV operations in dynamic marine environments. Unlike traditional methods, this approach introduces a dual-metric collision risk prediction framework by integrating the distance to the closest point of approach (DCPA) and the time to the closest point of approach (TCPA), enabling real-time and accurate risk assessment. To address the challenges of over-exploration and suboptimal convergence in the model predictive path integral (MPPI) algorithm under fixed sampling kernel conditions, we propose a diffusion-inspired annealing algorithm. This method adaptively adjusts the perturbation kernel, achieving a dynamic balance between exploration and convergence, thus significantly enhancing path optimization efficiency. Simulation experiments demonstrate the superiority of the proposed strategy, achieving not only precise collision and obstacle avoidance but also enabling scalable, cooperative multi-AUV operations across highly complex and variable marine scenarios. The proposed method contributes to improving autonomous navigation strategies for underwater robotics, offering effective solutions for multi-AUV operations in challenging scenarios. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
