	abstract = {This chapter examines human–robot collaboration in multi-robot environments, addressing critical challenges in supervising multiple autonomous agents simultaneously. We explore shared autonomy models that balance human oversight with robot independence, interface design principles for managing cognitive load and ensuring scalability, and methods for maintaining situation awareness across distributed robot fleets. Key topics include human-in-the-loop versus human-on-the-loop control architectures, trust calibration mechanisms, safety protocols, and explainable AI for transparent decision-making. Natural interaction methods encompassing voice commands, gesture control, and augmented reality interfaces are analyzed as scalable supervision tools. Real-world case studies from manufacturing, warehouse automation, and search and rescue operations demonstrate practical implementations, revealing substantial performance improvements including significant capacity gains in manufacturing operations and highly efficient operator-to-robot ratios in logistics environments. The chapter identifies research gaps in extreme-scale supervision, multi-robot trust dynamics, and ethical frameworks while examining emerging technologies such as brain-computer interfaces and mixed reality systems that will shape future human-multi-robot interaction, providing actionable guidance for designing systems that enhance human capabilities while ensuring safety and effectiveness across industrial applications. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
