	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105026441205?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This study addresses the critical challenge of demand forecasting for High-Cost Repairable Life-Limited Parts (HCR-LLPs) in aviation logistics, such as engines and hydraulic pumps, which are characterized by high economic value, safety implications, and complex lifecycle constraints. Traditional methods - including mathematical analytical models leveraging Markov chains and system simulations employing discrete-event techniques - struggle with computational complexity and fail to dynamically capture stochastic uncertainties inherent in repair cycles and supply processes. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel multi-agent simulation (MAS) framework integrated with a genetic algorithm (GA) for optimizing spare part inventory. The MAS models key entities (e.g., aircraft, repair facilities, production plants) as autonomous agents, simulating part turnover via Monte Carlo methods while incorporating generalized probability distributions for unscheduled failures and repair durations. The GA minimizes inventory quantity subject to dual operational constraints: a minimum fill rate and maximum aircraft grounding rate. Validated through a case study of 30 aircraft (60 engines), the model converged to an optimal inventory of 31 spare engines over 100 GA iterations. Multi-run simulations confirmed robustness, achieving a 93.33% fill rate and 2.4% grounding rate - significantly outperforming traditional methods. Sensitivity analysis revealed vulnerability to repair-cycle extensions (+10% repair time increased inventory to 35 units) but resilience to scrapping-rate fluctuations. This research demonstrates that integrating MAS with GA effectively balances inventory costs and mission readiness in stochastic HCR-LLP supply chains, offering a scalable framework for military and commercial aviation logistics.  © COPYRIGHT SPIE. Downloading of the abstract is permitted for personal use only.},
