	abstract = {In the smart manufacturing ecosystem of the future, there will be many dispersed industrial devices that communicate wirelessly with each other in real time and must compete for limited bandwidth on shared wireless frequencies. It introduces a new way to manage wireless communication in smart factories, by combining a multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL)-based framework with intelligent channel allocation, edge-cloud computing, and blockchain technology for logging purposes. It discusses how operational data from various types of industry sources are processed in the smart factory environment through the actions of multiple autonomous intelligent agents that develop policies on how to allocate and utilise channels. The decisions made by the agents regarding channel allocation are implemented at the edge server, allowing real-time communication and access to processed system data through the cloud server for centralised analysis and monitoring purposes. The approach also incorporates blockchain technology to create an immutable record of every communication and allocation event, thus ensuring transparency and integrity with respect to all system data. The multi-agent reinforcement learning setup was tested using both value-based and policy-based learning methods. It showed that the value-based approach performed well in earning rewards and experienced fewer communication conflicts, while the policy-based approach learned more smoothly and achieved better task completion, though with slightly more collisions. Overall, both learning methods delivered similar performance, but they differed in how quickly and stably they learned. The learning trends clearly showed the contrast between value-based and policy-gradient approaches. Alongside this, the blockchain component continuously recorded system activities, ensuring that all communications remained secure, traceable, and trustworthy throughout the operation  © 2026 IEEE.},
