	abstract = {The rapid growth of wireless technologies for short-range communication highlights the critical need for efficient resource management in Cellular Vehicle-to-Everything (C-V2X) networks. Operating within the Fifth Generation (5G) technology framework, these networks improve system performance through direct device-to-device interaction and resource allocation among mobile users and proximate devices. However, effectively allocating resources in V2X networks remains a challenging issue with current methods. This study introduces Dynamic Resource Reservation with Deep Reinforcement Learning (DR2-DRL), a novel approach leveraging a multi-agent deep reinforcement learning framework to mitigate packet collisions in vehicular networks. DR2-DRL aids vehicles in making intelligent radio resource selections. An enhanced attention-based technique is applied to increase training efficiency, enabling vehicles to systematically focus on pertinent data from the observations and actions of nearby vehicles. With its ability to select resources independently without the need to provide global information, this approach is especially wellsuited for C-V2X. Comprehensive simulations reveal that DR2DRL performs better than alternative decentralized techniques, proving its reliability and scalability in constantly changing vehicular networks. DR2-DRL achieves a 5.26% improvement for a platoon size of 8, highlighting its ability to provide more up-to-date information in urban scenarios compared to Modified MADDPGwitTDec. © 2025, International Association of Engineers. All rights reserved.},
