	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105015856614?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Researchers have widely studied the formation control for heterogeneous multi-agent systems due to the diversity of applications that can be found. Of course, it is essential to highlight that agents must avoid colliding with other agents or objects in the environment in which they operate. In this sense, this work tackles the collision-free formation control problem in a perturbed heterogeneous multi-agent system formed by first- and second-order agents. Collision avoidance is addressed using the Repulsive Vector Fields (RVF) approach. It is shown mathematically that the agents do not reach a deadlock while performing their motion. Therefore, the distance between the agents is always greater than the safety distance, avoiding collisions. The Generalized Proportional–Integral Observer (GPIO) is implemented to estimate the external perturbations of the model, and the classic Active Disturbance Rejection Control (ADRC) is used to solve the formation control. A numerical simulation comparison with Artificial Potential Functions (APF) presents the proposed approach's advantages. Furthermore, real-time experiments exhibit the performance of the collision avoidance method. © 2025 The Author(s)},
