	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048977717?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {To address the challenges of intense resource competition among communication, sensing, and control in industrial wireless networks, where communication congestion in large-scale intelligent clusters thus limits production throughput, this paper proposes a Two-stage Adaptive Resource Management (TARM) method for integrated sensing, communication, and control (ISCC) systems. In the first stage, focusing on automated guided vehicle (AGV) material transportation within automotive assembly workshops, a safety-guided multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) adaptive closed-loop interaction cycle method is proposed. The base station (BS) proactively harvests sensing data to dynamically reconstruct AGV kinematic states. By dynamically modulating the closed-loop interaction cycles of multiple AGVs, the system shifts from traditional fixed-period communication to an active on-demand paradigm, this transition significantly reduces the underlying communication load, thereby effectively elevating the upper bound of the controllable AGV cluster capacity. In the second stage, leveraging the expanded upper bound of the controllable cluster scale from the first stage, this paper derives a cooperative scheduling algorithm for multiple AGVs. By incorporating AGV kinematic characteristics and cluster expansion capabilities with production efficiency, the algorithm dynamically adjusts task allocation logic to achieve a precise matching between logistical supply efficiency and multi-line demands, thereby enhancing workshop throughput. Simulation results demonstrate that TARM substantially outperforms the traditional algorithm with nmax outage tolerance (TANOT), classic ISAC algorithm (CISACA), and traditional algorithm (TA) benchmarks, achieving a 56% reduction in global communication load and a throughput enhancement of at least 30.73%. © 2026 IEEE.},
