	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048701813?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Construction machinery in human-machine collaborative environments requires real-time dynamic obstacle avoidance capabilities. To meet this demand, we propose a deep learning-based intelligent trajectory planning approach. This approach takes as input a forty-four dimensional feature vector comprising real-time pose data of engineering equipment, target operation points, and dynamic obstacles at construction sites. A seven-layer fully connected neural network (FCNN) establishes a mapping model from high-dimensional space to continuous path points, utilizing ReLU activation functions and batch normalization techniques to accelerate training convergence. To overcome the limitation of traditional algorithms getting trapped in local optima under complex scenarios, we design a dynamic receptive field window mechanism that selects minimum potential energy path points in real time. Cubic Bézier curve segmentation is then applied for smooth trajectory optimization. In simulated multi-agent interaction scenarios representing construction sites with up to 20 obstacles, our method achieves a 98% search success rate within a 20-s timeout constraint, with an average response time of only 0.16 s—representing efficiency improvements of 95.9% and 98.8% compared to enhanced artificial potential field methods and RRT algorithms, respectively. By deeply integrating data-driven deep learning advantages with classical motion planning theory, this solution supports key human-robot collaboration scenarios including grinding robots and concrete distributor machines performing dynamic yielding maneuvers. It provides a novel technical pathway for safe obstacle avoidance in smart construction site human-machine symbiosis operations. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
