	abstract = {In recent years, autonomous mobile robots have been deployed in various locations because of such factors as labor shortages. These robots require the ability to efficiently navigate to their destinations, yet many hazards lurk in the environments where they operate. Deviating from pre-generated paths or temporarily stopping to avoid dangers may sometimes make smooth travel impossible. Therefore, the present study was aimed at improving path planning methods by defining speed-restricted zones and adding constraints such as blocked sections according to the surrounding conditions and the travel path. We demonstrate the effectiveness of this method for shortest path planning using cost functions within multi-agent system environments. Based on various simulations conducted using an environmental map simulating a small-scale restaurant, we report the construction of a prototype system capable of assisting robots to navigate autonomously more safely and efficiently. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
