	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044304188?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {A proof-of-concept for a blockchain-secured vehicular monitoring system is presented, in which driver alcohol concentration is detected, vehicles are geolocated, and impairment is predicted from physiological signals. The architecture integrates a Modular Sensor Battery (BSM) for breath alcohol detection, a GPS module for precise positioning, and an IoT Fitbit Smart Band Bracelet (IFSB) for continuous heart rate (BPM) and blood oxygen saturation (SpO₂) monitoring. All data streams are was transmitted to a Data Collection Platform (CD Platform), with cryptographic hashes stored on an Ethereum blockchain to ensure integrity and tamper resistance. Controlled driving simulator trials were revealed strong correlations between alcohol concentration and physiological metrics (BPM: ρ = 0.83; SpO₂: ρ = −0.88). The collected dataset was partitioned into training and testing subsets and used to train predictive machine learning models, enabling robust evaluation of system performance. Predictive modeling with Random Forest and Extreme Gradient Boosting was achieved high accuracy, explaining a substantial share of variance in alcohol levels from non-invasive physiological measurements. These results demonstrated the feasibility of enhancing direct sensor readings with physiological prediction, providing a robust, scalable, and secure framework for road safety, fleet monitoring, and occupational health applications. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
