	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042465440?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Recent studies identify BLE and ZigBee as promising candidates for low power, short range multi node architectures, while Wi-Fi and UWB can deliver higher through put and lower latency with careful electromagnetic and reliability optimization. NFC based and hybrid approaches are also gaining interest for secure data readout, second life battery applications, and maintenance without continuous wireless connectivity. Beyond telemetry, next generation WBMS platforms are integrating cloud connectivity, digital twins, and machine learning algorithms to enable predictive diagnostics, remaining useful life estimation, and over the air (OTA) parameter optimization. These capabilities improve state of charge (SOC) and state of health (SOH) estimation accuracy while enabling fleet level monitoring and continuous, software defined performance enhancement throughout the battery lifecycle. However, the deployment of wireless BMS introduces new design challenges concerning cyber security, functional safety, synchronization, and coexistence with other in vehicle wireless systems, along with the need for standardized, automotive qualified communication protocols and certification frameworks. This paper surveys the state-of-the-art developments in wireless communication for EV BMS, encompassing industrial implementations, experimental demonstrators, and research proposals up to 2025. It provides a comparative analysis of BLE, ZigBee, Wi-Fi, UWB, LoRa, and NFC technologies in terms of performance, scalability, cost, and safety implications. On this basis, the study identifies application oriented technology choices and highlights promising hybrid and cloud integrated architectures that are shaping the future evolution of BMS in electric vehicles. © 2026 IEEE.},
