	abstract = {This paper addresses the problem of integrated resource management at a container terminal under conditions of high vessel handling intensity. A multifactor mathematical model for synchronizing a group of quay cranes operating on shared rails is developed and theoretically substantiated. Unlike existing solutions, the model integrates dynamic nonintersection constraints and ground transport fleet capacity with real-time vessel hydrostatics and stability parameters within a single multi-stream architecture. This optimization ensures that high-intensity combined loading and unloading operations strictly adhere to safety margins for the vessel's vertical center of gravity, heel, and trim. Special attention is paid to the concept of 'vertical slot', where optimizing the order of access to the vessel's cells minimizes idle movements of the crane trolley. The model's study based on three cranes confirmed the possibility of reducing the total vessel handling time and minimizing ballast water pumping requirements. This is achieved by eliminating mutual locking of equipment, preventing violations of the vessel's stability and equalizing the load on the terminal's logistics network. The proposed approach improves coordination efficiency of quay crane operations, reduces idle time, and enhances overall terminal throughput, demonstrating its practical applicability for decision support in container terminal management under uncertainty. © 2026 IEEE.},
