	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046950293?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In response to the technical bottlenecks faced by existing underwater robots when performing tasks in complex and dynamic unknown sea areas, such as the single-point failure risk of centralized planning systems in complex environments and the lack of global collaborative cognition in single-machine decision-making, this paper studies and proposes a collaborative task planning and decision-making optimization method based on the integration of distributed knowledge graphs and multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL), aiming to enhance the safety, robustness, and collaborative efficiency of underwater robot groups in real-time task allocation and obstacle avoidance. Firstly, this paper proposes a distributed knowledge graph construction method for underwater operation environments, integrating multi-source heterogeneous information. Secondly, a scene-aware communication topology strategy based on local perception is designed, which enables on-demand interaction of key knowledge according to the real-time relative situation of the robot group. Finally, the extracted structured collaborative knowledge is used as a prior constraint for the multi-agent reinforcement learning network, and through a distributed reward mechanism and action masking technology, the model is guided to generate safe, compliant, and globally optimal planning strategies. Ultimately, simulation experiments show that in typical exploration tasks, the communication cost of the proposed distributed collaborative decision-making framework is significantly reduced by approximately 90%, and the task completion rate is increased to 94.5%.  © 2026 IEEE.},
