	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048719377?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Cooperative path planning for underwater multi-robot systems remains challenging in dynamic and uncertain environments because of time-varying ocean currents, sensor noise, communication constraints, and collision risks among neighboring robots. This paper presents DM-CPP, a diffusion-based cooperative path planning framework for underwater spherical multi-robot systems. First, a two-dimensional task-oriented model is established to represent robot kinematics, hydrodynamic disturbances, and sensing uncertainty in typical constant-depth underwater missions. Then, a multi-agent conditional diffusion framework is developed to generate collision-free paths in parallel by conditioning on robot states, target positions, obstacle distributions, and inter-robot coordination constraints. To improve robustness under dynamic perturbations, an interaction-aware noise prediction network is introduced to explicitly encode local robot interactions and environmental disturbances. In addition, an adaptive replanning trigger is introduced to balance responsiveness and replanning cost under limited-bandwidth communication, together with an incremental denoising strategy for efficient local path updates. Simulation results show that DM-CPP outperforms A*, RRT*, and an RL baseline in planning efficiency, path smoothness, safety, and cooperative robustness, achieving 0.08 rad/m curvature variation, 98.7% safety compliance, and low communication load.  © 2026 IEEE.},
