	title = {Autonomous Agentic Edge Operating Systems: Architectural Frameworks for Decentralised Multi-Agent Coordination and Real-Time Hardware Interfacing},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {2026 4th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Applications: Healthcare and Internet of Things, AIMLA 2026},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/AIMLA67915.2026.11522276},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041632284?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The issue of implementing AI agents on cloud servers is causing more issues, which the majority of people do not discuss openly. The latency is excessive, the dependability is not high enough, and in any respect of safety use it is practically unserviceable at all. Our paper is on the Agentic Edge OS-an operating system which we created ourselves based on a blank embedded board such as the Raspberry Pi 4B, to execute AI agents directly on its embedded edge hardware. To create a system that does all its thinking without the help of a cloud server, we simply created a simple Buildroot-based Linux image and coordination protocols of the Multi-Agent System. The system is based on systemd controlled Python runtime, Redis shared state and direct access to hardware via Python GPIO and PWM. Another thing that not many people have tried previously is letting agents read plain English instructions and figure out hardware wiring and GPIO mappings automatically. On our test, the edge approach is about 160 times faster than cloud configurations, as well as 1.79× faster than kernel scheduling. These figures indicate clearly that edge running intelligence is highly sensible to the robotics, industrial control, and smarter infrastructure systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
