	doi = {10.1109/SCECS69812.2026.11566059},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044135532?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {To address the communication bottlenecks, severe tether-induced drag, and inadequate navigational autonomy of traditional Remotely Operated Vehicles (ROVs) in marine monitoring, this paper proposes an edge-cloud collaborative USV-ROV system. Utilizing the USV as a mobile edge relay, the architecture integrates a force-feedback smart winch to achieve Autonomous Launch and Recovery (ALARS) and active tether tension control. For autonomous navigation, we construct a multi-agent framework combining 2D cross-product-based reactive obstacle avoidance with global dynamic target allocation, ensuring collision-free operations in constrained waters. Furthermore, an asymmetric hierarchical perception mechanism is developed: a lightweight YOLO model is executed at the edge (ROV) to ensure ultra-low latency for real-time tracking and reactive control, while a high-precision YOLO model - integrated with Vision-Language Models (VLMs) - is deployed in the cloud for complex semantic parsing and high-fidelity obstacle classification. Experimental results demonstrate that the system effectively suppresses tether tension, enhances navigational safety, and achieves robust visual perception, providing a highly reliable solution for autonomous marine monitoring. © 2026 IEEE.},
