	abstract = {The multi-agent pickup and delivery (MAPD) problem involves coordinating agents to repeatedly execute pickup and delivery tasks without collisions or deadlocks. The majority of existing approaches assume well-formed environments with numerous pickup and delivery locations, such as automated warehouses designed to carry robots. However, these assumptions are unrealistic in constrained environments with few task endpoints, such as construction sites with limited pickup and delivery locations. To address this issue, several studies have introduced orientation to the environment to reduce collisions and improve performance. Conventional methods do not address how orientation is determined, although it impacts the overall performance significantly. Therefore, we propose a genetic algorithmbased orientation optimization method based on the distributed planning and asynchronous execution (DPAE) framework, which is adaptive to constrained environments. By determining the edge orientations, the proposed method enhances the coordination efficiency over DPAE while preserving the MAPD problem completeness. Our experimental results demonstrate that our method achieves shorter makespans and reduces travel distances and detours among agents compared to existing baselines, thereby improving the overall performance. © 2026 IEEE.},
