	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043753706?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Enabling non expert users to control robotic systems through natural language represents an important step toward democratizing access to advanced automation. This paper presents a modular framework that integrates Large Language Models (LLMs) with multi robot control using the Model Context Protocol (MCP). A central contribution of this work is the proposal of an Agent-to-Agent (A2A) architecture designed to bridge the gap between probabilistic semantic reasoning and deterministic robotic control. By assigning distinct responsibilities to specialized agents (specifically for planning, execution, and verification) the system decouples high level intent from real time kinematic constraints. Operating within an asynchronous event-driven loop, these agents coordinate to invoke safety aware tools (e.g., takeoff, waypoint navigation) via MCP, allowing users to issue intuitive commands like "form a line along the X-axis."Validated within a containerized ROS 2 and Crazyswarm2 simulation environment, our results highlight the potential of this distributed architecture to lower the barrier to entry in swarm robotics, enabling domain experts to focus on mission goals rather than implementation details. © 2026 IEEE.},
