	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049363063?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {An important branch of machine learning that focuses on how agents should behave in each environment to maximize cumulative rewards is called rein forcement learning (RL). In the last 10 years, RL has moved from theoretical study to real-world application in various sectors, propelled by growing processing capacity, the accessibility of massive amounts of data, and developments in deep learning. In addition to showing RL’s revolutionary significance across disciplines and suggesting important prospects, this comprehensive overview synthesizes recent achievements in the field. Markov decision processes, policy optimization, value-based techniques (such as Q-learning), and model-based versus model-free methodologies are among the fundamental ideas of RL initially examined in this study. After that, it explores important innovations like AlphaZero, soft actor-critic, proximal policy optimization, trust region policy optimization, and deep Q-networks. As demonstrated by ments in robotics, autonomous cars, recommender systems, and gaming (such as Atari, Go, and StarCraft II), integration with deep learning has made it possible for RL agents to function effectively in high-dimensional, unstructured settings. Recent velopments, such as offline RL, multi-agent RL, hierarchical RL, and safe RL are also examined in the study. These paradigms have greatly improved the resilience, ability, and safety of RL agents in intricate, unpredictable contexts. With case studies showing its promise in automated trading systems, personalized treatment planning, and dynamic resource allocation, the application of RL in healthcare, finance, tics, and natural language processing is also growing. Despite these achievements, eral problems remain, including the need for interpretable decision-making, incentive sparsity, the trade-off between exploration and exploitation, and sample inefficiency. Safety, task generality, ethical alignment, and fairness must also be addressed for real-world deployment. The overview cites recent research that attempts to get around these limitations by combining RL with massive language models and causal inference and through curricular learning, transfer learning, and meta-learning. The chapter concludes by outlining new directions for the future, such as neuro-symbolic RL, quantum RL, and the convergence of RL with generative models like diffusion models and large-scale foundation models. Another interesting approach is incorpo rating human input via RL with human input, particularly when bringing AI agents into line with human ideals. The necessity of interdisciplinary cooperation and mark creation to direct the appropriate development of RL technologies is sized in the review’s conclusion. RL has the potential to become a key component of the design of intelligent, autonomous systems in the twenty-first century through going innovation and critical examination. © 2026 Walter de Gruyter GmbH, Genthiner Straße 13, 10785 Berlin, Germany.},
