	pages = {58 - 65},
	doi = {10.1016/j.procs.2026.08.008},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105052775944?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Cobots have been installed in a lot of places for flexible manufacturing, but folks still can't quite get the teamwork right: workers often misunderstand what the system is doing while it's going. Sure, the safety rules and how it's controlled work like they're supposed to, but working together gets worse if the operator thinks wrong about the robot's moves and plans. This paper tries to fix that by using ideas from multi-agent systems and explainable AI (XAI). The main point is that explanations should actually be part of coordinating, not just tacked on after the fact. Research out in factories shows operators build their understanding from bits of behaviour, little stops, changing speed, slight shifts in path, and teamwork stays good when these bits give them the same impression. When the picture gets fuzzy, things get more uncertain, even if everything else is working okay. To organise this discussion, the paper offers a five-tier system linking how much is explained to how stable the understanding is, how much thinking it takes, and how interventions happen. Every tier is looked at with clues from studies about people working with robots and automation. Findings consistently lead to one conclusion: an explanation's effectiveness hinges not on information volume, but on how its timing, proportionality, and framing align with the operator's interpretive state at delivery. The paper concludes by acknowledging limitations of a conceptual approach, pinpointing needs for empirical validation, and suggesting impacts for system design and AI governance. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.. All rights reserved.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
