	abstract = {This study investigates hazardous driving behaviours among commercial truck drivers, the associated behavioural drivers, and their relative influence on driving risk. A mixed-method approach was employed, combining structured literature review, expert interviews, and structured surveys administered to commercial truck drivers. The dataset comprised responses from 62 truck drivers and 24 industry professionals. The collected data were analysed to evaluate the influence of behavioural drivers, the likelihood and severity of risky driving behaviours, the availability of threshold values and technological add-ons, and the key challenges faced by truck drivers. Based on these findings, conceptual frameworks were developed to support the identification and mitigation of transport-related hazards from both organizational and individual perspectives. The results indicate that driving experience, organizational safety guidelines, and prior accident involvement exert the strongest influence. Among the identified behaviours, distracted driving, slow driving in inappropriate lanes, and fatigued driving were found to be the most frequently reported.  © 2026 IEEE.},
