	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105053784404?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Autonomous UAV navigation in complex environments has attracted increasing attention in recent years due to its wide range of applications in robotics and intelligent transportation systems. However, vision-based UAV control in partially observable and dynamic environments remains challenging because of limited perception and the need to coordinate multiple control dimensions simultaneously. This paper proposes a reinforcement learning approach for such scenarios. Traditional methods typically employ a single, monolithic policy to jointly control throttle, roll, pitch, and yaw, which often leads to complex representations and degraded performance under partial observability. To address this issue, we introduce a decoupled policy framework that decomposes the UAV action space into separate control dimensions, reformulating the problem as a multi-agent setting. Each dimension is handled by an independent policy with a shared feature backbone, and coordinated through a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) communication mechanism to capture temporal dependencies. Experiments conducted in the photorealistic Flightmare simulator on obstacle avoidance, target tracking, and object search tasks demonstrate consistent performance gains over strong baselines. Specifically, the proposed method improves task success rates by approximately 10∼25%, reduces collision rates by up to 30%, and achieves faster convergence during training. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2026.},
