	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037833221?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The commercialization of distributed Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) logistics, particularly Beyond Visual Line of Sight (BVLOS) operations, requires addressing regulatory compliance, autonomous scalability, and system assurance. This paper presents the Drone Tree Network (DTN), a hierarchical cyberphysical framework designed to support performance-based BVLOS operations aligned with emerging Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) Part 108 requirements. The DTN integrates a cognitive decision stack combining a Weighted Sum Model for mission prioritization with an enforced emergency dispatch threshold and a hybrid Lazy Theta and D Lite path-planning architecture for real-time rerouting. Payload release safety is ensured through a dual-authorization protocol requiring both cryptographic validation using a FIPS 140-3-compliant hardware module and physical ground verification via a convolutional neural network and PointNet-based sensor fusion model, generating a ground-stability confidence score. Command-andcontrol resilience is strengthened through frequency-hopping spread spectrum and Unscented Kalman Filter-based positioning integrity. Simulation-based validation demonstrates measurable improvements in path efficiency, command-and-control robustness under jamming conditions, and fleet scalability, providing empirical evidence toward scalable BVLOS-compliant UAV logistics. © 2026 IEEE.},
