	abstract = {To achieve quick situational evaluation in the disaster areas where communication does not always work, and the conditions of the terrain develop unpredictably, the swarm coordination of unmanned aerial vehicles is an indispensable part of the situational assessment. The proposed work offers a swarm coordination framework that provides reinforcementbased learning, which is linked to multi-agent value decomposition, adaptive reward shaping, and decentralized trajectory optimization, to perform real-time mapping and target localization. The system uses the heterogeneous UAV sensing data, spatiotemporal state fusion, and policy-based cooperative path planning to ensure the stable operation of the system under dynamic constraints. The experimental assessment showed 34% quicker regional coverage, 28% reduced localization error, 41% higher real-time map consistency, 32% decreased inter-UAV collision risk, and 29% inter-UAV collision risk reduction in comparison with the current coordination strategies. The framework was also able to operate steadily with communication bandwidth limitations and platform heterogeneity. The findings suggest that using reinforcement learning can be used to achieve resilient, high-precision swarm behavior that can be deployed to a complex disaster-response context, providing a scalable platform for next-generation autonomous aerial coordination. © 2026 IEEE.},
