	abstract = {Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) for fixed-wing aerial robots requires both aerodynamic fidelity and high-throughput simulation, yet existing platforms typically provide either simplified kinematics or CPU-bound high-fidelity models. We present Planax, a JAX/XLA-accelerated, GPU-resident benchmark framework that combines nonlinear six-degree-of-freedom (6-DOF) dynamics with wind-tunnel-derived F-16 aerodynamic lookup tables using tensorized interpolation. On a single NVIDIA A100 GPU, Planax achieves 2.33 \times 10^{7} environment steps per second for independent heading/pitch/velocity rollouts and supports batch sizes of up to 2 \times 10^{7} independent 6-DOF instances. These figures characterize independent rollouts; interacting formation and pursuit-evasion tasks additionally incur relative-geometry costs. Under matched model, interpolation, and rollout settings, the XLA-compiled JAX Tensor-LUT backend achieves lower steady-state latency than the evaluated same-model PyTorch baseline across the tested components and batch sizes. Cross-fidelity multi-agent ablations, with all policies evaluated in the high-fidelity environment, show that high-fidelity training improves both task performance and safety. Across three seeds, the pursuit-evasion win rate increases from 0.534\pm 0.097 to 1.000\pm 0.000, while formation slot RMSE decreases from 1493\pm 130 m to 445\pm 211 m and overload incidence decreases from 0.480\pm 0.049 to 0.067\pm 0.071. The open-source Planax codebase and video demonstrations are available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/Aeroplanax-2087. © 2016 IEEE.},
