	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048028455?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Collective behavior is often modeled as the outcome of interactions among similar agents, while variation among individuals is commonly treated as a property that agents bring into the group. Here, we take a complementary view: Individual differences can also be produced, amplified, or reorganized by the collective conditions in which agents interact. We tested this idea using phototactic robots under global light and robot-mounted local light cues. The robots shared the same controller and sensorimotor design, but they retained small physical differences that affected their motion. Global light increased inter-individual behavioral-state differentiation, whereas local light cues reduced these differences. Within trials, the light environment modulated the prevalence of both milling-dominant and high-switching robots. These results show that light-mediated collective conditions changed not only how strongly robots differed from one another, but also what kinds of behavioral profiles appeared within the group. This system provides a simple physical platform for studying how environmental structure, local interaction, and embodied variability shape behavioral differentiation in collectives. © 2026 The Author(s). Advanced Intelligent Systems published by Wiley-VCH GmbH.},
