	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036119979?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Multi-Agent Pathfinding (MAPF) ensures collision-free motion for teams of autonomous robots, yet practical applications also demand intelligent distribution of tasks across those robots. We present a fully integrated framework that couples optimal path planning with dependency-aware task allocation and validated from graph-based models to simulation and real-world robots. First, we develop scalable MAPF algorithms in a graph abstraction and study their complexity, makespan and sum-of-costs behaviour. These algorithms are then embedded in a ROS 2 + Gazebo environment that incorporates realistic kinematics, network delays and partial observability; Cartographer provides on-line occupancy grids while the Nav2 stack supplies local/global planners. To elevate the system from'just moving' to'moving with purpose', we formulate task assignment with precedence constraints as a combinatorial optimization problem and solve it using an Ant Colony Optimization (ACO) meta-heuristic that minimizes total completion time and idle time of the robot. The same software stack is ported to differential drive robots equipped with 2-D LiDAR and Raspberry Pi controllers. By demonstrating end-to-end transfer from theory to real robots, this work establishes a reproducible pathway for deploying cooperative, task-driven multi-robot systems in manufacturing, logistics, and smart-city scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.},
