	abstract = {We demonstrate a novel edge-centric multi-robot path planner algorithm, Zone-MAPF, that can be easily parallelized & executed on separate compute cores in an edge server to deliver low computational latency and scalability and is well-suited for edge robotics systems wherein robot planning and control algorithms are driven from a central edge network. Our approach deconstructs large complex maps into small zones and plans collision-free paths in each zone concurrently. Our planner operates in two modes: a start-up mode where path plans are generated for robot fleets at rest and a continuous lifelong planner mode where successive path plans are generated as and when robots complete their current tasks. Our planner uses a graph-based two-way search algorithm to find optimal robot paths on sparse waypoints which makes our multi-robot planner a grid-free approach that promises to work on any map. We have demonstrated our zone-MAPF planner in simulation across multiple maps. We also demonstrate our Zone-MAPF planner in a Gazebo lab map, integrated with the Nav2 stack deployed with two TB3 Burger robots. Based on our simulation results, the success rate of our zone-based planner is excellent (∼ 90% - 98%) which is significantly higher than that of a prior art grid-based planner by as much as ∼ 5.8X for identical robot configurations. The computational latency of the collision solver in our continuous planner is low (∼ 10ms per robot) which is well-suited for real-time planning of individual robots from the edge.  © 2025 IEEE.},
