	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105015210576?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Multi-agent path finding (MAPF) has been widely used for many real-world problems, such as warehouse automation. Reinforcement learning (RL) has been employed to develop partially observable distributed MAPF algorithms that can be scaled to any number of agents or map sizes. However, the RL-based MAPF policy may cause oscillation behavior, deadlock and detours, which can lead to the failure of MAPF. This paper introduces R3, a new hybrid policy framework for MAPF, in which the RL-based policy is incorporated with the random walk policy and the reverse resumable A*-based policy. When an agent exhibits oscillation behavior or falls into a deadlock, the random walk policy helps the agent escape the trouble. When no other agents are within the agent's field of vision, we utilize the reverse resumable A*-based policy to assist the agent in avoiding any potential detours. This paper uses the state-of-the-art RL-based MAPF algorithm, Decision Causal Communication, to do the simulation experiments on eight benchmark grid maps. The results show that R3 can significantly improve the performance of the baseline Decision Causal Communication algorithm.  © 2025 IEEE.},
