	abstract = {The Multi-agent systems (MAS) like fleets of warehouse robots are increasingly applied to complex situations, but their safety is of high concern owing to the computational expense involved in verifying neural controllers. Classical verification techniques are likely to suffer from slow performance or even infeasibility for large-scale MAS, which can result in safety concerns and operational inefficiencies. The current research remedies this by presenting a new scaleable symmetry-reduced verification technique which identifies agents with comparable behaviors and merges them into a reduced representative 'quotient' model. The reduced model is verified for safety with mathematically warranted bounds to ensure the representative outcome characteristic of the full system. In the case of safety failure, only the representative agents are retrained in a verification-directed loop, updating the whole system without the need for exhaustive computation. The system gains significant verification time reduction while still ensuring high safety levels shown by realistic warehouse configurations, and hence, it is a candidate for efficient and reliable MAS deployment. © 2025 IEEE.},
