	abstract = {Power transformers are vital assets for energy utility companies and industrial operators, necessitating effective management to ensure high availability, extended lifespan, and cost-effective operations.However, existing diagnostic methods often only provide a single value, such as a "Health Index", lacking specificity for stakeholders responsible for fleet management from different perspectives, such as operational costs (OPEX) and investment expenditures (CAPEX). Moreover, conventional methodologies take the operating age of transformers exclusively into account and place significant emphasis on this factor for e.g. replacement decisions, which can often lead to distorted results.This paper presents a comprehensive approach for transformer condition assessment, utilizing a two-dimensional methodology enabling holistic optimization of the decision-making process in asset management. The two-dimensional methodology considers the information from various diagnostic analyses and handles them in an objective way. Furthermore, it covers a possible solution for dealing with "data uncertainty", which is essential during the decision process in asset management.Results from a real project involving a power transformer fleet in a German power utility are presented to illustrate the practical implications of the two-dimensional approach. Applying the two-dimensional condition assessment methodology, critical units within a larger transformer fleet were identified during an initial evaluation. To reduce data uncertainty, targeted measurements were subsequently conducted. Finally, during an internal inspection and further diagnosis, the reason for the existing failure mode could be identified and repaired.The paper emphasizes that asset management decisions derived from condition assessment and data uncertainty analyses are crucial for reducing failure risk, consequently increasing the overall reliability of the transformer fleet. By addressing data uncertainty, asset managers can make informed decisions regarding replacement, repair, and proactive lifetime extension measures. © 2025 IEEE.},
