	abstract = {This paper presents a label-light, data-driven framework for risk assessment and prioritization of 150 / 20 kV (kilovolt) power transformers in Indonesia's Java-Bali grid. Conventional practice (Risk = Impact × Likelihood) is hindered by scarce, non-stationary failure data that make asset-level likelihood difficult to calibrate. Empirical risk groups are derived using KMeans clustering over a multi-domain feature set (condition, loading, consequence, exposure), and supervised classifiers are subsequently trained to reproduce these labels for fast, repeatable fleet-scale s coring. U sing a P erusahaan L istrik N egara (PLN)like mixed dataset (N = 800) with real-style structured features and a partially augmented sentiment channel, k ϵin 3,4,5 were scanned and k=4 was selected based on within-cluster sum of squares (WCSS), Silhouette coefficient, a nd Calinski-Harabasz (CH) criteria. The resulting clusters align with actionable profiles (low-risk modern, mid-life/high-load, aging/unreliable, highconsequence). On a held-out test set, the Random Forest (RF) model achieves 0.94 accuracy and 0.94 macro-F1 score, outperforming Logistic Regression (LR) and linear Support Vector Machine (SVM), with feature importance highlighting health index, age, maintenance cost, operational load, and criticality as dominant drivers. The proposed framework yields transparent label profiles a nd a uditable d rivers t hat m apd irectly t o maintenance, monitoring, and capital expenditure (CAPEX) policies, and complements reliability studies when explicit probability-offailure estimates are required.  © 2025 IEEE.},
