	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036026955?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Fighter aircraft, essential for defense and attack, rely on ejection seats as a critical safety measure to save pilots in emergencies. The Brazilian Air Force (FAB) uses Martin Baker systems in its fleet (F-5EM, AMX A-1, A-29, and T-27), including an oxygen regulator with a shear pin, which releases oxygen after ejection. To reduce costs and ensure self-sufficiency, the São Paulo Material Depot (PAMASP) is working to internalize its production based on technical specifications from the Air Force Logistics Center (CELOG). The study involved material analysis, CNC machining process adaptation, prototype production, dimensional and shear load testing, and comparison with original parts. Seven certified components were used as control group. Preliminary results indicate that despite the parts complexity and lengthy CNC setup, manufacturing is feasible with high precision and reliability, potentially saving over US$120,000 annually. This work outlines the steps to internalize the shear pin production, ensuring crew safety and operational readiness for FAB aircraft. Copyright © 2025 by ASME.},
