	doi = {10.1109/ICoIAS69065.2025.00019},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037434703?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Robotic and autonomous systems (RAS) offer a promising approach to overcoming the limitations of traditional human-driven logistics in large scale emergency and disaster response. This work presents a computational platform for orchestrating fleets of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) and unmanned ground vehicles (UGVs) to enable rapid, adaptive deployment in uncertain, hazardous environments. The system integrates modules for data integration, fleet modeling, optimization, resilience analytics, and human in the loop control, creating a unified architecture capable of supporting both fully autonomous and semi-Autonomous operations. In a simulated anthrax dispersion scenario over the Atlanta metropolitan area, UAVs perform reconnaissance and plume modeling, while UGVs deliver push packages, protective gear, and medical countermeasures, as well as transport patients and perform decontamination. The optimization engine dynamically assigns routes, vehicle tasks, and resource allocations, adapting to blocked roads, evolving hazard zones, and variable demand. The model prioritizes minimizing mission time, communication cost, and risk exposure. Simulation results demonstrate that this coordinated robotic network achieves substantial performance improvements over conventional approaches: first responders receive prophylactic therapies significantly earlier, supply and resupply reliability exceeds 99%, and PODs (Points of Dispensing) become operational far sooner. Under modeled assumptions, the system realizes up to 50% more lives saved relative to baseline strategies. This work contributes a methodological integration of geospatial modeling, agent-based simulation, stochastic optimization, and human-machine coordination, packaged in a modular and extensible framework. While the results are promising, challenges remain in model realism, operator trust, regulatory acceptance, and real-world validation. Future work will focus on field trials, robustness under sensor and communication failures, and the refinement of human-robot interaction mechanisms to support adoption in real emergency settings.  © 2025 IEEE.},
