	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/AIHCIR67580.2025.11405413},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035989047?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Addressing shortcomings in traditional UAV swarm systems concerning central dependency, single-point fault tolerance, and dynamic obstacle avoidance, this paper proposes a distributed control framework based on a pigeon-inspired hierarchical mechanism. This aims to enhance autonomous coordination and interference resistance within intelligent robotic swarms. By simulating pigeon flocks' hierarchical leadership and backup behaviours, an autonomous fault-tolerant leader-follower model is constructed, enabling rapid hierarchical restructuring and task relay in multi-agent cooperation. For obstacle avoidance, an "obstacle gap angle optimisation strategy"is proposed, guiding the swarm through complex environments by real-time evaluation and selection of optimal passage gaps. The system employs a lightweight communication architecture built upon the Robot Operating System (ROS), integrated with a proprietary low-power Bluetooth monitoring terminal. Experimental results demonstrate a 36.2% improvement in formation reconfiguration speed and a 12.5%reduction in path turning points, validating the proposed biomimetic control strategy's effectiveness and engineering utility in enhancing swarm robustness, collaborative efficiency, and environmental adaptability. © 2025 IEEE.},
