	abstract = {Driven by the booming low-altitude economy, aerial robots are increasingly required to have intelligent decision-making skills for complex indoor and outdoor scenarios. Simulator-based policy learning has become a standard approach to ensure training safety and reduce development costs. However, current learning environments for aerial robots have predominantly focused on Multi-rotor Aerial Robots, with limited attention given to buoyancy-based platforms, such as blimps. To bridge this gap, this paper presents a novel simulation framework that utilizes universal rigid-body components to model a blimp's dynamics accurately. Moreover, a multi-agent learning interface was designed to support policy training and validation. To verify the simulator's dynamic modeling accuracy, the inverted pose stabilization task was conducted, where the blimp firstly reached and then maintained an upside-down orientation. Four agents were used to train the position control policy, demonstrating the learning interface and system framework. The paper aims to offer a virtual environment that can be customized for various simulation needs, thereby facilitating future research and development of miniature indoor blimps.  © 2025 IEEE.},
