	abstract = {Efficient exploration is a critical component of Multi-Robot Systems (MRS) deployed in safety-critical domains such as disaster response, planetary mining, and infrastructure inspection. In heterogeneous Multi-Robot Task Allocation (MRTA) systems, the search strategy used for the exploration of resources or targets can significantly influence overall mission performance. This paper investigates the impact of three distinct search strategies, random walk, systematic flower pattern search, and spiral outward search, on the effectiveness of a hierarchically decentralized heterogeneous MRTA system developed in our previous works. Using pyrobosim, a 2D simulation environment, modeled after NASA's Space Robotics Challenge Phase 2, we evaluated how each search strategy affects key performance metrics including time to resource discovery, auction open times, task completion time, and total travel distance. Our findings show that the choice of search strategy substantially influences the coordination dynamics and mission efficiency. The results inform best practices for the deployment of search behaviors in decentralized robotic teams tasked with exploration, rescue, or resource localization. © 2025 IEEE.},
