	abstract = {This study introduces a reinforcement learning-driven framework for scheduling in smart manufacturing systems that integrate autonomous mobile robots (AMRs) and collaborative robots (cobots). The methodology models complex industrial environments by incorporating dynamic states such as energy use, queue lengths, collision risks, and synchronization delays. Actions are optimized through adaptive task allocation, while reward functions balance throughput, energy efficiency, and safety. Learning is enabled using deep and policy gradient approaches to handle large-scale state spaces, ensuring scalability, adaptability, and resilience. Experimental findings reveal that reinforcement learning-based scheduling consistently outperforms rule-based, heuristic, and evolutionary approaches. Compared to traditional methods, the proposed hybrid RL+ optimization approach reduces task completion time by over 20%, minimizes downtime, and enhances collision avoidance by more than 15%. The system also achieves up to 94% adaptability and 95% overall productivity while consuming less energy per task. These outcomes confirm that reinforcement learning is not just an incremental step but a transformative shift in industrial scheduling, capable of harmonizing efficiency, safety, and sustainability in Industry 4.0 settings. © 2025 IEEE.},
