	doi = {10.1109/IoTaIS67227.2025.11282059},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031587628?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper proposes a deep reinforcement learning (DRL)-based flocking control framework for quadcopter swarms operating in obstacle-dense environments with varying swarm sizes. Conventional rule-based methods rely on predefined mathematical heuristics, which limit their adaptability and scalability, particularly in scenarios with high obstacle density or large agent populations. In contrast, the proposed approach formulates the flocking task as a partially observable Markov decision process (POMDP), integrating local perception and communication constraints for each agent. The control policy is trained using Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization (MAPPO) with centralized training and decentralized execution. The reward function incorporates Boids-inspired rules (separation, cohesion, alignment) together with explicit collision and corridor constraints. Extensive simulations involving 5, 20, and 50 agents show that the proposed method consistently achieves at least a 96% goal-arrival rate across all scenarios, while maintaining agent and obstacle collision rates below 2% and 6%, respectively, even for large-scale swarms. These results demonstrate superior scalability and robustness compared with both rule-based and state-of-the-art MADDPG baselines, making the proposed framework a promising solution for practical decentralized UAV swarm control.  © 2025 IEEE.},
