	doi = {10.1109/MEDCOM67532.2025.11405017},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036697036?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper reports decentralized control of two autonomous mobile robots (AMRs) operating simultaneously in a cluttered arena. The first AMR follows a color-coded track and modulates speed/turning based on local color cues; the second performs extended-range obstacle avoidance using onboard sensing. Each robot treats the other as a moving obstacle - no shared map, no coordinator, and no inter-robot messaging are used. Experiments in a 3×3 m2 environment with static obstacles and randomized initial poses yielded 19/20 collision-free runs (95%). The single failure was traced to a missed ultrasonic detection. Velocity and heading logs show that deviations from references coincide with intentional avoidance or color-triggered actions rather than controller instability. These results indicate that simple, low-latency local policies can support safe, simultaneous AMR operation at lab scale. The conditions under which centralized coordination would add value (dense fleets, narrow aisles, strict takt time) are discussed, and a hybrid path is outlined in which a lightweight dispatcher complements the validated decentralized safety layer.  © 2025 IEEE.},
