	abstract = {Customary manufacturing systems are intrinsically rigid and slow to react to changes in their operating environment. Multi-agent systems based on LLMs such as GPT-4 allow humans and machines to interact using natural language processing, improving the system's coordination and adaptability to the environment. These designs use special agents (Resource Agents (machine-level) and Product Agents (decision level)) that utilize LLMs to comprehend high-level objectives, assign tasks (for instance, creating G-code), and negotiate independently. These agents work well in highly customized contexts, such as small-batch manufacturing. Incorporating dynamic activation, real-time task decomposition, and a structured communication protocol can greatly improve their collaborative efforts. In case studies, LLMs have 100% accuracy when the input is simple and 86% accuracy when it is complex. Their performance degrades when they hallucinate, or make incorrect API calls. There are still issues of safety, correctness, and scalability, and other research includes applying them to alternative manufacturing methods, alternative datasets, and optimizing agent communication. This model shift, by combining human flexibility and machine efficiency, creates a new type of agile production.  © 2025 IEEE.},
