	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105002885628?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The global air cargo industry has grown significantly over the past decade, driven by e-commerce expansion, demand for faster shipping, and efficient logistics. As expectations for quick deliveries rise, airlines and cargo operators face pressure to expand fleets and offer cost-effective, flexible solutions. One key strategy to meet these demands is passenger-to-freighter (P2F) conversion, which enhances cargo capacity without the high costs of new freighters. P2F conversions are a cost-effective and environmentally friendly alternative to purchasing new freighters. By repurposing older aircraft, airlines extend their operational life, reduce costs, and increase fleet flexibility. This approach has become critical as e-commerce fuels rising air cargo demand. Converted freighters support various needs, from long-haul routes requiring high payload capacities to regional routes demanding flexibility and frequent flights. They enable operators to customize fleets for specific requirements, adapting to diverse cargo and route demands. Freighter conversions provide significant operational benefits beyond cost savings. Airlines can modify aircraft to transport general goods, high-value items, perishable and specialized cargo. Key structural changes include reinforcing fuselages and floors for heavier loads, installing larger cargo doors, and removing passenger amenities. These modifications ensure safety compliance and optimize aircraft for cargo use. The study highlights differences between long-haul and regional freighter conversions. Long-haul models are designed for substantial payloads and long distances, making them ideal for international routes. In contrast, regional models prioritize fuel efficiency, faster turnaround times, and flexibility for frequent short-distance operations. By examining the strengths and limitations of each, the presentation offers insights into their practical applications across varied operational contexts. The presentation provides a detailed comparison of popular aircraft freighter conversions, focusing on five key performance metrics: payload capacity, range, fuel efficiency, cargo handling systems, and turnaround time. These metrics are analysed to choose the most suitable freighter model for cargo transport needs. © 2025 IACAS 2025 - 64th Israel Annual Conference on Aerospace Sciences. All rights reserved.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 0}
}

