	journal = {Proceedings - Frontiers in Education Conference, FIE},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/FIE63693.2025.11328671},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033004001?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This WIP Innovative Practice paper explores the integration of technical learning outcomes with emotionally resonant experiences in a first-year robotics course using a swarm of synchronizing firefly robots. These robots are individually programmed but synchronize the periodic flashing of their lights as a community. Students have described these displays of emergent behavior as 'magical' and as generating fostering positive emotions. We hypothesize these emotions can help students develop a sense of community while supporting their ability to acquire technical skills in the lab. The programming of each robot affects the emergent behavior of the swarm, enabling a strong coupling between computational and emotional aspects of the learning experience. This preliminary paper outlines a short sequence of weekly lab sessions in which these robots will be introduced, forming part of a first-year engineering design and robotics course at the University of Michigan. A preliminary assessment scheme that draws from engineering education literature to analyze subjective data, with a particular focus on student interviews and reflections is also described. A pilot offering is planned for September 2025 with results to be presented at FIE in November. Conference participants will also have the opportunity to interact with a small firefly swarm and discuss how emotional elements might be helpful in other educational contexts. © 2025 IEEE.},
