	doi = {10.1109/ICUS66297.2025.11294344},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031882705?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper investigates the task allocation and planning problem for multi-agent systems with safe-return constraints under temporal logic specifications. First, a hybrid task definition framework integrating top-down global tasks with bottom-up local safe-return tasks is proposed. This framework ensures that agents achieve global task objectives while safely returning. Then, based on poset product algorithm, a new concept called "pending subtask"is introduced, which enables rapid task decomposition and efficiently solves the combination of multiple complex LTL specifications. Further, an extended mixed-integer linear programming (e-MILP) method is designed for offline optimal task allocation, and an online planning algorithm embedding a dynamic obstacle avoidance strategy is developed, ensuring that agents can flexibly handle time uncertainties during task execution while avoiding collisions. Finally, the proposed method is validated through a simulation example of a courier logistic factory. The results demonstrate its high efficiency and robustness in complex task decomposition, cooperative task allocation, and real-time planning for multi-agent systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
