	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105019116187?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The advent of industrial revolutions has dramatically transformed global manufacturing processes, with the fourth industrial revolution introducing digital manufacturing (DM) and digital twin (DT) technologies. Digitization of manufacturing includes the application of 3D CAD, simulation and analytics that enable the efficiency of production processes, ensure minimum wastage, and improve the quality of the end product. At the same time, railways have developed in terms of design, efficiency, and comfort of the passengers which demands new solutions to further increase the efficiency, safety, and sustainability of the systems. The introduction of DT has remarkable contribution to the development of railway systems. DTs are accurate virtual counterparts of physical systems that exist and constantly receive data from sensors and other sources. DTs have constant updates, predictive maintenance, and improved designs which most simulations do not possess. This technology is becoming more applicable to the railway industry and enables operators to perform scenario simulation and fleet management, energy management and regulatory compliance. DTs enhance maintenance planning and operations by predicting the likelihood of failures reducing idle time thus enhancing operational effectiveness and leading to the creation of safe, reliable, and environmentally friendly railway systems. This chapter examines the role played by DTs in railways compared with the rest of the simulation models and elaborates on their potential uses in different railway systems. The enhancement of the design processes by DTs goes further to the enhancement of operational safety, enhancement of the passenger experience, and the cost efficiency of the systems. © 2026 selection and editorial matter, V. M. Ravindra Kumar, K. P. Vineesh and Sunil Kumar Sharma; individual chapters, the contributors.},
