	doi = {10.1109/MRAI65197.2025.11135857},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105017974089?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Multi-Agent Path Finding (MARF) is a critical and indispensable component of multi-agent systems. For multi-agent systems, the optimal path is defined as the one that best meets the design objectives among all potential paths. In this study, the Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization (MAPPO) algorithm is utilized to address the path-finding problem for multi-agents in deep underground environments. Through the design of the observation space, reward function, and other components, agents are incentivized to reuse paths previously explored by other agents during the path-finding process, leading to a substantial reduction in excavation costs. In the harsh and energy-constrained conditions of deep underground environments, reducing excavation costs is of paramount importance. This strategy not only increases task completion rates but also enhances the safety of the paths. In simulation experiments, the path excavation rate is significantly reduced, while the reuse rate is improved, demonstrating the effectiveness of the proposed approach. © 2025 IEEE.},
