	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031768671?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper explores decentralized multi-robot path planning using deep learning. Inspired by recent advancements in distributed deep learning, we designed a framework in which each robot communicates solely with its local neighbors to collaboratively compute collision-free paths. Throughout our approach, each robot encodes its local observations, extracts valuable features using Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN), aggregates the extracted features using Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) and structured graphbased message passing. Finally, efficient and informed decision-making is performed using multilayer perception (MLP) to predict conflict-free actions for each robot based on shared contextual information. The proposed approach enhances system scalability, robustness, and reduces communication overhead without centralized coordination. It achieves 97.6% Accuracy, and 98.9% success rate. A Hardware prototype is implemented and tested showing that the proposed system holds promise for real-world applications such as autonomous warehouse management and coordinated robotic exploration, contributing to the advancement of intelligent and cooperative multi-agent systems.  © 2025 IEEE.},
