	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030056671?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Electrical vehicle (EV) powertrains, which include motors, inverters, gears, and batteries, are essential for speed, safety, and economy. Analysing high-resolution multivariate sensor information in real-time is necessary to guarantee early defect identification in these subsystems. This paper suggests a deep learning (DL)-based defect detection system that automatically learns temporal and spatial patterns from unprocessed sensor streams by combining Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) with Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks. The procedure consists of reliable data collection from heat, voltage/current, and accelerometer sensors, and then preprocessing stages such signal segmentation, noise filtering, and normalisation. The hybrid CNN-LSTM model uses automatic feature extraction as well as sequential pattern recognition to better classify faults than standard methods that rely on features that are designed by hand and short models. The suggested model does much better than standard methods like Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and XGBoost, as shown by experiments on real-world EV datasets. It has an AUC-ROC value greater than 0.98, an accuracy of 96%, a precision of 94.3%, a recall of 96.1%, and an accuracy of 94.3%. The model's ability to generalise across different working situations and find small problems with few false positives is what makes these improvements attainable. Designed for edge deployment, the system guarantees real-time processing capabilities. This study shows that the hybrid CNN-LSTM technique, which offers high accuracy, real-time reactivity, as well as scalability for fleet-level applications, sets a new state-of-the-art standard for defect identification in EV powertrain systems.  © 2025 IEEE.},
