	abstract = {Efficient path planning is critical for Robotic Mobile Fulfillment Systems (RMFS), in which multiple robots must navigate dynamically without collisions. This study evaluates five multi-agent path-finding (MAPF) algorithms: WHCA*, FAR, CBS, ST-SPF, and STMS in simulated RMFS environments with varying map sizes (7 × 7 to 11 × 11) and agent densities (5 to 9 agents). The performance was assessed using the makespan and sum of costs. The results show that CBS and ST-SPF consistently outperformed the other algorithms. CBS excels in mid and spaceful environments despite its robust conflict resolution capabilities, whereas ST-SPF demonstrates superior scalability and adaptability in crowded, semi-crowded, and semi-spaceful environments. In contrast, WHCA∗ and FAR struggled with structured congestion, and STMS did not show any significant advantage because of its hybrid overhead and reliance on randomness. These findings suggest that CBS is more stable in every RMFS environment, as indicated by its low makespan standard deviation, whereas ST-SPF is better suited for dynamic settings with higher density. This study provides practical insights for selecting MAPF algorithms for real-world warehouse automation, highlighting the importance of conflict resolution and environmental adaptability. © 2025 IEEE.},
