	abstract = {This paper studies autonomous distributed cooperative monitoring of aerial robots moving at unknown speed using a multi-fixed-wing UAV (FW-UAV) system. The monitoring problem is addressed through a distributed cooperative control scheme that integrates adaptive formation restructuring and fencing maneuvers. The proposed observer-based distributed cooperative control (OB-DCC) framework uses formation and fencing strategies to ensure continuous tracking and containment of the target while maintaining inter-UAV coordination and collision avoidance. In addition, the proposed OB-DCC framework incorporates a vector field-based collision avoidance mechanism to enhance its robustness. Initially, a distributed position observer estimates the real-time target trajectory, which allows the FWUAVs to adjust the formation dynamically. An auxiliary state is introduced to handle uncertainties in target velocity estimation error and improve accuracy. The proposed OB-DCC framework considers the FW-UAV system dynamics, including aerodynamic limitations, kinematic constraints, and multi-agent communication topology. In addition, the fencing controller ensures uniform distribution of FW-UAVs around the target, preventing escape while minimizing energy consumption. The simulation results demonstrate the satisfactory performance of the proposed OBDCC method in maintaining persistent monitoring of UMO while optimizing FW-UAV resource allocation.  © 2025 IEEE.},
