	abstract = {As Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), especially delivery drones, become popular in academic and civil sectors, needs for collision prevention is unavoidable yet lacking. This paper introduces a machine learning and simulator based method to optimize safety distance for swarm cargo drones. Which addresses airspace safety and efficiency challenges in the delivery UAV fleet. The onboard system could compute and predict the safety distance between UAVs and obstacles under different weather conditions by integrating machine learning with simulated UAV models with aerodynamic parameters. The presented method utilized a simulator-based reinforcement learning framework, giving a simulation environment and evaluation process. The integrated system retrieves the historical weather data from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) database for both training and validation purposes. Key features include a modified Iris drone model for various missions and an improved reinforcement learning model focusing on wind/gust speed, wind/gust directions, and cargo weight. The simulation is powered by the Robot Operation System (ROS) network and Gazebo simulator, which provides a realistic simulation environment to create an accurate model representing real wind data. © 2024, American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics Inc, AIAA. All rights reserved.},
