	doi = {10.1115/OMAE2023-101707},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85173586478?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In 2021, the Norwegian fishing fleet emitted approximately 855 000 tonnes of carbon dioxide-equivalents, which accounts for 1.3% of the total emissions in Norway. Converting to zero and low emission fuels is one of the measures that can significantly reduce GHG emissions of the fishing fleet. This study investigates the possibilities for using hydrogen and ammonia-fed fuel cells for powering a coastal fishing vessel (a 13 m longliner). The study is part of a Norwegian project where several stakeholders and research and industrial entities collaborated. General arrangements for the vessel are sketched for various trip lengths and configurations of power system using hydrogen, ammonia, batteries, and diesel. The effect of using alternative fuels on dimensions, specifications and stability are evaluated. Safety aspects for the vessel and bunkering are assessed. Using mathematical optimisation, the economic aspects of using alternative fuels onboard the vessel are studied. Finally, the environmental aspects of adopting alternative fuels on similar vessels and requirements for the infrastructure in ports are discussed. The results show that ammonia has the highest potential for reducing emissions for long trips; however, there are technical, economic and safety challenges with using ammonia. It is concluded that hydrogen-driven fishing vessels are feasible, but the Norwegian fish quota system, which among other aspects is based on vessel length, creates limitations for fuel storage and thus longer ranges. © 2023 American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME). All rights reserved.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
