	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85189906376?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {One critical component towards enabling the ecosystem for Advanced Air Mobility (AAM) is airspace congestion management. Differentiating from current commercial air traffic, AAM is inclusive of a wide variety of use cases, users, and uncrewed aerial system (UAS) vehicle classes, along with a much larger range of geographical scope (urban to regional). Developing effective approaches for airspace congestion management will allow for higher operational densities without sacrificing safety nor efficiency. With this motivation in mind, we study the setting where UAS fleet operator attempts to find optimal routings for its drones while subjected to a congestion pricing scheme provided by an airspace manager. We formulate the interconnected optimal routing and optimal congestion pricing problem, and propose a reinforcement learning-based algorithm to obtain a desirable congestion pricing scheme. We apply our proposed algorithm to two case studies, one representing a capacitated airspace scenario, and the other depicting logistics operations with multiple demand profiles. We highlight the improvements shown by our learning-driven algorithm over several benchmarks, as well as discuss limitations in our study (e.g., homogeneity of drone priorities). © 2023, American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics Inc, AIAA. All rights reserved.},
