	abstract = {Identifying the shortest path is an extensively studied optimization goal across various application domains. With the increasing prevalence of automation approaches in industrial settings, there is a growing need to solve problems containing similarities efficiently. This involves not only identifying collision-free paths in two-dimensional grids, but also more complex geometries in three dimensions, and transferring knowledge within a defined degree of similarity. In this paper, spherical 3D CAD structures are incorporated into a multi-agent pathfinding problem using transfer learning. To solve this optimization problem, a multi-agent deep reinforcement learning method using 3D CNNs is applied. The research analyzes the impact of the learning strategy, the necessary number of few-shot learning iterations, and the training's reproducibility. The presented work demonstrates the possibility of enhancing the transfer between geometries with a certain degree of similarity. When applying this in practical scenarios, a trade-off exists between path optimality and the computational effort required for learning from scratch. © 2024 IEEE.},
