	abstract = {The nuclear industry has expressed interest in the expanded use of wireless technologies in nuclear power plant (NPP) applications for both the existing fleet as well as future designs. Such uses include industrial control systems (ICS) and data acquisition monitoring systems on or near safety-related/important-to-safety (SR/ITS) systems, or near critical digital assets (CDAs). The Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC), as the licensing and regulatory agency for US civilian NPPs, needs further insights on the expanded use of wireless communication technologies to better understand how they may adversely interact with existing or future SR/ITS systems, and thus challenge a plant’s ability to maintain a safe operational state. Current safety guidance only addresses the EMI/RFI considerations and thus presents a generalized approach for evaluating the use of wireless technologies. More complex wireless implementations and configurations as well as use of advanced technologies could create radio-frequency (RF) environments that current information and guidance does not address. Current NRC research efforts are investigating the development of possible guidance updates and additional information to support the evaluation and planning for wireless implementations . This effort is investigating not only analytically-based results but also testing data to validate and confirm the analytical findings. In addition, as complexity of wireless implementations increases, the methods available for calculated solutions decrease while the required effort increases. Thus, additional means of evaluating testing data and developing appropriate means for applying these results to different implementation scenarios is a supplemental goal of this research effort. This paper discusses one aspect of a research effort on wireless technologies at the NRC. Physical separation from an RF emitter provides one means to protect SR/ITS systems from electromagnetic interference/radio-frequency interference (EMI/RFI). NRC guidance, specifically Regulatory Guide (RG) RG 1.180, Rev. 2, “Guidelines for Evaluating Electromagnetic and Radio-Frequency Interference in Safety-Related Instrumentation and Control Systems,” contains an equation that can be used to determine the separation distance to reduce the effects of EMI/RFI. The equation was developed to address EMI/RFI concerns from all types of RF emitters. There are indications that this equation may provide a larger than necessary separation distance for modern wireless devices due to this fact. As part of the ongoing research effort, an initial set of tests were performed, and the data analyzed, to evaluate this possibility. This paper discusses the testing effort, preliminary findings, and future steps. © 2023 American Nuclear Society, Incorporated.},
