	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85184962885?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In 2019, the French government modified its mid-term plutonium management policy, with the introduction of a milestone to demonstrate the industrial feasibility of the plutonium multi-recycling in PWRs, while preserving the capability to introduce a FR fleet in late 21st century. The MIX (MOX fuel with enriched uranium matrix), one of the most promising fuel assemblies design, is considered here. This advanced fuel assembly concept need optimisation to meet targeted requirements or drivers including nuclear safety, economic viability, and sustainability. This set of criteria, sometimes contradictory, can make the optimisation work complex and challenging. An innovative multi-objectives optimisation method based on kriging, the SHADOC-based Design Development System (SDDS), was previously developed by EDF for FR design applications. This methodology was newly adapted to PWR core calculation, based on the APOLLO2 spectral code and the COCAGNE core simulation code. Each fuel assembly is defined by its design parameters and its core fuel performances are evaluated. This paper aims to quantify the potential of plutonium multi-recycling in a 100 % MIX-loaded EPR-type core through the following study: an U-235 enrichment varying from 1 %w to 4 %w, an average plutonium enrichment from 6 %w to 10 %w, a Pu-239 fraction from 34 %Pu to 56 %Pu and a Pu-241 fraction from 2 %Pu to 12 %Pu. © 2022 Proceedings of the International Conference on Physics of Reactors, PHYSOR 2022. All Rights Reserved.},
