	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85183468538?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The US Department of Energy Light Water Reactor Sustainability program engages in research to improve the economics, reliability, safety, and lifetime of the light water reactor fleet. Economics and reliability are complimentary topics where progress has certainly been made, but an effective tool to evaluate economic risk and guide future decisions has not been available. One area where the potential exists for development of a useful tool is referred to as generation risk assessment, which uses many of the techniques associated with probabilistic risk assessment, but with a focus on top events where power plant electricity generation is impacted. One of the primary obstacles to the adoption of generation risk assessment is the effort involved in development of a useful model followed by lack of an automated process for parametric study. A new tool called the Versatile Economic Risk Tool (VERT) has been created to overcome the challenges of generation risk assessment. VERT is designed to allow study of equipment reliability and degradation from an electricity generation perspective by coupling fault tree analysis software and statistical analysis software that supports repetitive analyses for parametric study. This paper provides a description of VERT and provides demonstration calculation results to show the potential usefulness of the tool. © 2021 PSA. All Rights Reserved.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
