	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/79959395177?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Space debris mitigation guidelines have been issued in the past years by the United Nations and other international institutions, and were adopted by many of the space-faring nations. However, these guidelines alone are no longer sufficient to control debris growth and protect access to the desirable orbits. The current density of orbital debris is such that it will continue to increase with time even if all launch activities are stopped. This will eventually lead to a collision cascade that will render some orbits unusable. The only way to tackle this problem is to actively remove ten to fifteen large objects per year from specific orbits to prevent any collisions from cascading. These actions would guarantee safe access to space for future generations. The concept of a company, named RetroSpace, and a system for active space debris removal have been developed from a detailed analysis including an identification of the critical stakeholders, their needs and expectations, the current markets, the legal and regulatory framework, the available and proposed technologies. A range of technologies were assessed and the final end-to-end design has been justified in terms of its validity, maturity, cost and risk. Leveraging robotic technology from the German Aerospace Centre (DLR), a fleet of spacecraft will target and remove large debris from the low earth orbits in which most catastrophic collisions are predicted to occur and place them into safe re-entry orbits. The system is designed to be cost-effective and commercially responsive, while minimizing the net risk of collisions and stabilizing the debris population. The ground segment will provide the necessary operational control and full transparency to customers and stakeholders during all phases of operations. The system is conceived to operate within existing commercial, legal and regulatory framework with the lowest possible risk to third parties. The business implementation has also been investigated and assessed. The proposed solution provides value for all parties involved. Copyright ©2010 by the Technische Universiteit Delft and Deutsches Zentrum für Luftund Raumfahrt. Published by the IAF.},
