	volume = {2011-September},
	pages = {},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85044721594?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In oil and gas industry, the possession of updated data location of the vessels is a critical item for optimization of predefined activities of the vessels, in order to reduce travel time and so the costs involved. A few years ago, Petrobras has began the use of Fleet Management System (SGF, from portuguese), which is based on GNSS positioning system and transmission of the coordinates via UHF signal. This system has limitations as the range about 22 nmi (40 km) and a hardware and software complex architecture requirement on-board. To make this situation more favorable, the Subsea Service Unit of Petrobras (E&P-SERV/US-SUB) is implementing other technologies for tracking and monitoring their vessels, integrating these technologies into a single solution. These technologies are: Automatic Identification System (AIS), Inmarsat D, and internal network Multisat. The AIS is a primal safety equipment for vessels, which transmits its position via VHF signal and provides wider coverage area than SGF (approximately 30 nmi). Its use is simple and cheap, because the most of Petrobras vessels and platforms has the equipment, and it only requires the configuration of equipment to transmit the data to the onshore base. Another option is the communication of position data via Inmarsat D, which will be compulsory for all vessel contracts in US-SUB. This technology transmits position data via satellite communication network; this eliminates the problem of coverage area. In previous trials it has demonstrated high reliability and integrity. Position data from those different sources are processed and stored in a single solution. The use of multiple sources ensures huge reliability due to redundancy. This information feeds several applications, such as the Geographic Information System for Subsea Engineering (GIS-SUB) and control/monitoring panels. The systems support logistic analysts, so they can plan the best fleet schedules, achieving better results for the company. Copyright 2011, Brazilian Petroleum, Gas and Biofuels Institute - IBP},
