	abstract = {Flight safety can be improved through analyses of increasingly available aircraft and flight-related data that provide greater awareness of day-to-day flight operations and organizational safety culture. Analyses enable the identification and monitoring of previously unknown or unquantifiable events, trends, and individuals' safety attitudes and perceptions. However, translating vast amounts of raw data from disparate sources into meaningful information poses unique and substantial challenges. This paper presents an approach for data aggregation and display to transform raw data from multiple varied sources for a large aircraft fleet over an extended time period into unbiased, actionable information. The analysis approach consists of a statistical technique called Baseline Comparative Analysis coupled with rule-based composite indices for data fusion. Flight data from both fixed and rotary wing aircraft fleets were analyzed and combined with data from other sources, including mishap and hazard related data, flight statistics, and safety related data self-reported by aircrew personnel. Statistical baselines were developed from aggregated data representative of normal flight operations against which single flights could be assessed to identify the occurrence of anomalous flight performance. Results from the analysis of single flights were aggregated for a fleet-wide perspective and comparisons at the squadron level. The principal focus was to provide a methodology for identifying anomalies or changes in patterns at the squadron or unit level that may be indicative of potential unsafe actions or trends. Results are presented in a multi-tiered set of information displays. The comprehensive "Analyst Dashboard" is a hybrid of both numeric and graphic information that provides a detailed representation of data for multiple squadrons. The "Enterprise Manager's Display" differentiates squadrons through composite safety related indices derived from multiple data sources that provides decision makers with a comprehensive yet concise safety mosaic of the entire aircraft fleet. Analysis of data for a fleet consisting of 22 individual aircraft squadrons and more than 200 aircraft operating over tens of thousands of flight hours shows that objective differences and outliers can be identified from a large scale real-world dataset using this methodology.},
