	abstract = {Wireless technologies have increased efficiency in many industrial settings across the world by enabling a mobile workforce and improving communications. Unfortunately, the nuclear power industry has been slow to adopt wireless technology as a result of safety, security, and reliability concerns expressed by regulators and others. A Research and Development (R&D) effort is currently being conducted to address two significant technical concerns, wireless electromagnetic compatibility (EMC) and wireless coexistence, which are important for the introduction of wireless technology in nuclear power plants. Most equipment in the current fleet of nuclear power plants has never been tested for vulnerability to wireless transmission. As such, the impact of modern wireless devices on nuclear safety and plant reliability is not understood. Existing guidance from the Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC) and Electric Power Research Institute (EPRI) rely on the use of exclusion zones to protect plant equipment that can result in exclusion zones of up to eight feet for some tablet devices rendering them ineffective for the mobile workforce. Another unknown is how different wireless devices will operate in close proximity to one another in the harsh electromagnetic environment of a nuclear power plant. These issues are being addressed through laboratory experiments to characterize the attributes of wireless devices and in-plant evaluations to identify the vulnerabilities of plant equipment. Based upon the results of these investigations, test methods and equipment are being developed for laboratory qualification testing and in-situ measurements to determine the immunity of plant equipment to wireless signals and the coexistence of various wireless technologies in the plant environment. © 2015 by International Society of Automation - ISA. All rights reserved.},
