	pages = {},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84877285836?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {The CHESF - Companhia Hidro Elétrica do São Francisco - is a federal controlled company, the bigger electric enterprise in Brazil. Currently, it is composed of 14 hydroelectric generation plants and one thermoelectric, in a total of 10, 618.32MW. Its transmission system has 94 substations and more than 18, 00km of power lines, amongst 69, 138, 230 and 500kV. In order to unify and standardize the operation and maintenance management of all its assets, including telecommunication, computers, generators, power transformers, etc., CHESF has formulated the SIGA (Sistema Integrado de Gestão de Ativos - Asset Management Integrated System) - An Information System specifically designed to support its asset management and operation, as a whole. Being a public company and in accordance with the Brazilian law, the acquisition of the software was bided at an auction, which leaded to the cheapest IT solution considering all the specifications. In Forma Software (a local software house), based on Equipmaint", has supplied all the CHESF requirements for the SIGA project, and developed such a complex solution in two years, since the contract signature till the full acceptance into the electric system operation. Besides the large dimensions of CHESF, which covers a huge territorial area (above 1 million squared kilometers) and imposes a rational use of telecommunication networks (TCP/IP Wide Area Network), SIGA has many new customized features that easy strategic asset management in a complex matrix organization environment. It has been developed to allow and encourage the use of RCM (Reliability Centered Maintenance) and LCC (Life Cycle Cost), and supports SLA (Service Level Agreement) management and BI (Business Intelligence) tools. SIGA deploys an integrated database with all the equipment records, operation occurrences and the maintenance work orders. It also allows distinct hierarchies for the programmed maintenances, medium and long-term activities planning, performance and reliability indicators and hazards precautions. Particular user-assembled routines allow automatic work orders generation that takes into account the age, usage, particular characteristics of the equipments and even predictive diagnosis, such as thermo vision, vibration and chemical analysis. SIGA was designed to interface distinct teams, e.g. operation, predictive and corrective maintenance. That prevents waste of time, human errors, misunderstandings and administrative overturns. It is a pretty novelty in the power sector. Furthermore, it was designed in compliance with the regulatory agencies. SIGA also optimizes large stops in critical systems, such as generation machines and power lines, making possible to wisely schedule simultaneous work orders for several maintenance teams, one for each subsystem. This feature reduces costs related to unavailability into large power systems, such as generation plants, substations or transmission power lines. It is an interesting feature for industries which needs equipment replacement or refurbishment, above all, considering regulatory restructuring. An intensive work has been applied to make available its many useful innovative features at high customization level and low costs. It was, and still is, a great challenge for CHESF. This paper is intended to share the main experiences taken from the SIGA project.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 0}
}

