	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85116949704?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In the last years satellite navigation is becoming more and more useful for the realisation of services and applications where user mobility and related aspects are considered. On the other hand, it is a matter of fact that the future European Galileo system and the modernisation of GPS are driving and pushing Research and Development (R&D) activities toward unexplored fields so far, like finance and related security aspects. Those systems will be able to bring users into a new world of possible applications, where their position can be certified being computed on reliable data; this could be considered as a consequence of the introduction of EGNOS and the integrity message into the navigation data message broadcast by satellites. The project "GAL-PMI preceding Galileo: products and services to support mobility and security" is aimed at enhancing R&D, innovation and technology transfer towards companies and economic-production system in Piedmont, being issued within measure 3.4: "multi-axial interventions to support economic activity" (DOCUP 2000/2006) of Piedmont Region. GAL-PMI is promoted by Torino Wireless Foundation, Istituto Superiore Mario Boella (ISMB) and Politecnico di Torino as founding members of the GAL PMI consortium. It includes both Small and Medium Enterprises (SME), public and private research centres and university, deeply involved in R&D activities on telecommunications, infomobility and Global Navigation Satellite System (GNSS) sectors. The final goal of the GAL-PMI Project is to demonstrate feasibility of GNSS to enable the development of added-value applications in the fields of road charge, emergency and safety, and payment systems. Road charge field is oriented toward the realization of traffic management centres able to coordinate procedures like road pricing and parking lot reservation. Emergency and safety will includes services to improve safety and security of people by developing intelligent control centres for tracking of rescue teams and fleet management. In this context it is important to underline that this part will deal with professional communications systems like VHF, Tetra and the innovative DMR. Finally, the GAL-PMI project will try to demonstrate how GNSS can improve security aspects concerning payment systems. All these applications are based on a common technological infrastructure, which include user terminals, GNSS Local Element and middleware platform. The paper will give an introduction on the applications above mentioned; therefore, it will describe the architecture of the system developed within the project with particular focus on technology. For example advantages in using GNSS with the integration of terrestrial mobile networks will be faced, highlighting the role of EGNOS in providing integrity data and service guarantee. Finally, a general overview on the test phase will be provided.},
	type = {Conference paper},
	publication_stage = {Final},
	source = {Scopus},
	note = {Cited by: 3}
}

